at element (event bubbling from descendants) are synchronized when the element has hx-sync... Hmm, actually I need to be careful: the synchronization applies to requests that would be issued by the element that has hx-sync, and the selector determines the group. Let me recall the actual implementation:\n\nIn htmx source (2.x), `hx-sync` is handled in `shouldCancelRequest` / `issueRequest`: \n\n```js\nfunction shouldCancelRequest(elt, httpVerb, target) {\n    if (elt.hasAttribute('hx-sync')) {\n        const sync = elt.getAttribute('hx-sync')\n        const syncType = getInternalData(elt).boosted ? 'boosted' : httpVerb\n        // parse out the selector\n        const elements = sync.split(':')\n        const selector = elements[0].trim()\n        const strategy = elements[1] ? elements[1].trim() : 'drop'\n        const syncable = closest(elt, selector, elt)\n        if (syncable) {\n            ...\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, roughly: it uses `closest(elt, selector)` — the nearest ancestor of the issuing element that matches the selector. So `hx-sync=\"#log-drawer-body:replace\"` would look for an ancestor matching `#log-drawer-body` — the log links are NOT inside `#log-drawer-body`, so that fails. And it's evaluated on the requesting element.\n\nLet me actually check the vendored htmx source to be precise. There's a vendored htmx.min.js in static/.\n\nActually, rather than relying on my recollection of hx-sync mechanics, the robust and verifiable approach is the JS event-based fix in canopy.js:\n1. Track the requesting element / run id per log request.\n2. On a new log request (`htmx:beforeRequest` with `data-log-open` elt), abort any previous in-flight log request.\n3. Guard the swap: on `htmx:beforeSwap`, if the response belongs to a run different from the currently open one, cancel the swap.\n\nActually, let me look at htmx's API in the vendored version to confirm `htmx.trigger` and `htmx:abort` and `htmx:beforeSwap` use `event.detail.shouldSwap` / `event.