s:\n- `hx-sync=\"[selector]:[strategy]\"` where selector refers to the element(s) to sync with. If the current element is part of the sync group (matches the selector), then the strategy applies to requests made by this element, considering other requests in the group.\n- Strategies: `drop` (drop the new request if a request is in flight), `abort` (abort the in-flight request and let the new one proceed), `replace` (abort in-flight request, replace with new one), `queue first|last|all` (queue requests).\n\nCommon pattern: `hx-sync=\"this:replace\"` — for requests from the same element. For multiple elements sharing a target, pattern like `hx-sync=\"closest table:replace\"` isn't quite right either — you want, e.g., all log links to be one sync group.\n\nActually htmx docs show: `hx-sync=\"this:replace\"` on a form prevents a second submit from... and for multiple elements sharing a target: `<div hx-sync=\"this:replace\">` wrapping the links. If the links are inside a common wrapper and the wrapper has `hx-sync=\"this:replace\"`, then any request issued by descendants of that wrapper will be synchronized: an in-flight request from a descendant gets aborted when a new request from another descendant starts.\n\nBut our links are in `instance.html` which gets embedded into `page.html`. The log links live inside `<main id=\"main-content\">` presumably? Let me check the structure of instance.html and page.html fully. Actually simpler and more robust: implement the guard in `canopy.js` directly. The verdict says \"Add request serialization/abort or discard stale responses before swap.\"\n\nA clean JS approach using htmx's events:\n- Listen for `htmx:beforeRequest` when the request is for the log drawer (`event.detail.elt` closest `[data-log-open]` or the target is `#log-drawer-body`): abort any in-flight log request (`htmx:abort` event dispatched to the previous requesting element, or call `htmx.trigger(previousElt, 'htmx:abort')`).\n- Also, before swap, verify the response corresponds to the currently open run.\n\nAlternatively, use htmx config `htmx:configRequest`... The cleanest robust fix that directly maps to the verdict: \n\nOption A (JS abort on new request): keep a record of the last log request element; on new log request, abort the previous one. In htmx 2.x, you can send `htmx:abort` to an element to abort its in-flight request. On `htmx:beforeRequest` for log links, if `lastLogRequestElt` exists and differs, `htmx.trigger(lastLogRequestElt, 'htmx:abort')`. Then set `lastLogRequestElt = event.detail.elt`.\n\nOption B (discard stale responses): tag each request with the run id; in `htmx:beforeSwap` (or `htmx:oobBeforeSwap`), if the response's run id doesn't match the currently-open run (i.e., `lastTrigger`'s data), abort/discard.\n\nActually the most htmx-native approach is the hx-sync attribute. Let's check which htmx version is vendored. The verdict mentioned \"vendored HTMX 2.0.9\" in another verdict, so htmx 2.x. hx-sync exists in htmx 2.0.\n\nLet me think about using hx-sync with a shared target element. If each link has `hx-sync=\"#log-drawer-body:replace\"`, then: when link B issues a request, htmx looks for in-flight requests among elements that match `#log-drawer-body`? No — the selector selects the element whose requests are synchronized with this one. The docs example: \n\n```html\n<form hx-target=\"#content\" hx-sync=\"this:replace\">\n```\n\nand\n\n```html\n<div hx-sync=\"this:replace\">\n  <button hx-post=\"/update\">...</button>\n</div>\n```\n\nFor multiple buttons each with their own target, you put `hx-sync` on a shared ancestor: `<div hx-sync=\"this:replace\">`. But careful: `hx-sync=\"this:replace\"` on a div means requests from within that div... Actually per docs: \"The hx-sync attribute allows you to synchronize AJAX requests between multiple elements.\" The value is `<selector>:<strategy>` and if the selector is `this`, it refers to the element with the attribute. Requests originating from within that 