un={{.RunID}}\" hx-get=\"/logs?instance={{$.ID}}&amp;run={{.RunID}}\" hx-target=\"#log-drawer-body\" hx-swap=\"innerHTML\">open</a></td></tr>\n               {{end}}\n             </tbody></table>\n             </div>\ndiff --git a/templates/log.html b/templates/log.html\nindex d141ded..f163916 100644\n--- a/templates/log.html\n+++ b/templates/log.html\n@@ -1,10 +1,11 @@\n-<article id=\"log-{{.RunID}}\" class=\"log-drawer log-output\" aria-live=\"polite\">\n-  <div class=\"card-header\">\n-    <h3>Run log <span class=\"mono\">{{.RunID}}</span></h3>\n+<div class=\"log-pane\">\n+  <div class=\"log-pane-meta\">\n+    <span class=\"log-run-id mono\">{{.RunID}}</span>\n     {{if eq .State \"retained\"}}<span class=\"signal ok\">retained</span>{{else if eq .State \"evicted\"}}<span class=\"signal unknown\">not retained</span>{{else if eq .State \"unknown\"}}<span class=\"signal error\">unknown run</span>{{else}}<span class=\"signal error\">error</span>{{end}}\n+    {{if and (eq .State \"retained\") .Text}}<button type=\"button\" class=\"log-copy\" data-log-copy>Copy log</button>{{end}}\n   </div>\n   {{if eq .State \"retained\"}}\n-    {{if .Text}}<pre>{{.Text}}</pre>{{else}}<p class=\"muted\">The retained log is empty.</p>{{end}}\n+    {{if .Text}}<pre class=\"log-text\" tabindex=\"0\">{{.Text}}</pre>{{else}}<p class=\"muted\">The retained log is empty.</p>{{end}}\n     {{if .Exit}}<p class=\"muted\">completed with exit {{.Exit}}</p>{{end}}\n   {{else if eq .State \"evicted\"}}\n     <p class=\"muted\">This Run is known, but its log is no longer retained.</p>\n@@ -15,4 +16,4 @@\n     <p>Canopy could not read this Run log.</p>\n     {{if .Error}}<p class=\"muted\">{{.Error}}</p>{{end}}\n   {{end}}\n-</article>\n+</div>\ndiff --git a/templates/page.html b/templates/page.html\nindex d3011f7..4125582 100644\n--- a/templates/page.html\n+++ b/templates/page.html\n@@ -21,6 +21,17 @@\n       {{template \"instance.html\" .Selected}}\n     </main>\n   </div>\n+  <div id=\"log-drawer\" class=\"log-drawer\" role=\"dialog\" aria-modal=\"true\" aria-labelledby=\"log-drawer-title\" hidden>\n+    <div class=\"log-drawer-backdrop\" data-log-close aria-hidden=\"true\"></div>\n+    <section class=\"log-drawer-panel\" aria-label=\"Run log viewer\">\n+      <header class=\"log-drawer-header\">\n+        <h2 id=\"log-drawer-title\">Run log</h2>\n+        <button type=\"button\" class=\"log-drawer-close\" data-log-close aria-label=\"Close log viewer\">Close</button>\n+      </header>\n+      <div id=\"log-drawer-body\" class=\"log-drawer-body\"></div>\n+    </section>\n+  </div>\n   <script src=\"/static/htmx.min.js\" defer></script>\n+  <script src=\"/static/canopy.js\" defer></script>\n </body>\n </html>\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788550146887},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the current implementation. Let me confirm the details:\n\n1. In `instance.html`, each run's \"open log\" link has `data-log-open`, `hx-get=\"/logs?instance=...&run=...\"`, `hx-target=\"#log-drawer-body\"`, `hx-swap=\"innerHTML\"`.\n2. `page.html` has the `#log-drawer` panel with `#log-drawer-body`.\n3. `canopy.js`'s `openDrawer` sets `content.innerHTML = '<p class=\"muted\">Loading log…</p>'` and shows the panel.\n4. There's no `hx-sync`, no `hx-indicator` per request, and no request sequence guard. If the user opens run A then run B before A's response returns, A's slower response can arrive last and overwrite B's content in the drawer.\n\nThe verdict says: \"Add request serialization/abort or discard stale responses before swap.\"\n\nSo I need a fix. Options:\n1. Use `hx-sync` to serialize requests from the log links — e.g., add `hx-sync=\"this:replace\"` or place an `hx-sync=\"#log-drawer-body:replace\"`... Actually hx-sync syntax: `hx-sync=\"[selector]:[strategy]\"` — the selector identifies the element(s) to synchronize with. A common approach: on each log link, `hx-sync=\"closest #log-drawer-body:replace\"`? Hmm, hx-sync works within a group of elements. `hx-sync=\"this:replace\"` aborts the current request in favor of a new one on the same element. Since the links are different elements, we need a shared scope. \n\nActually simpler: the htmx approach for \"only one request at a time and newest wins\" within an element set is putting `hx-sync` on each request issuer pointing at a common ancestor or a common element. E.g., adding `hx-sync=\"#log-drawer-body:replace\"` on each link: when a link issues a request, htmx checks for in-flight requests on `#log-drawer-body`... hmm, actually hx-sync's selector matches against the element that would issue the request; the strategy applies to requests from those elements.\n\nLet me recall htmx 2.x hx-sync semantics:\n- `hx-sync=\"[selector]:[strategy]\"` where selector refers to the element(s) to sync with. If the current element is part of the sync group (matches the selector), then the strategy applies to requests made by this element, considering other requests in the group.\n- Strategies: `drop` (drop the new request if a request is in flight), `abort` (abort the in-flight request and let the new one proceed), `replace` (abort in-flight request, replace with new one), `queue first|last|all` (queue requests).\n\nCommon pattern: `hx-sync=\"this:replace\"` — for requests from the same element. For multiple elements sharing a target, pattern like `hx-sync=\"closest table:replace\"` isn't quite right either — you want, e.g., all log links to be one sync group.\n\nActually htmx docs show: `hx-sync=\"this:replace\"` on a form prevents a second submit from... and for multiple elements sharing a target: `<div hx-sync=\"this:replace\">` wrapping the links. If the links are inside a common wrapper and the wrapper has `hx-sync=\"this:replace\"`, then any request issued by descendants of that wrapper will be synchronized: an in-flight request from a descendant gets aborted when a new request from another descendant starts.\n\nBut our links are in `instance.html` which gets embedded into `page.html`. The log links live inside `<main id=\"main-content\">` presumably? Let me check the structure of instance.html and page.html fully. Actually simpler and more robust: implement the guard in `canopy.js` directly. The verdict says \"Add request serialization/abort or discard stale responses before swap.\"\n\nA clean JS approach using htmx's events:\n- Listen for `htmx:beforeRequest` when the request is for the log drawer (`event.detail.elt` closest `[data-log-open]` or the target is `#log-drawer-body`): abort any in-flight log request (`htmx:abort` event dispatched to the previous requesting element, or call `htmx.trigger(previousElt, 'htmx:abort')`).\n- Also, before swap, verify the response corresponds to the currently open run.\n\nAlternatively, use htmx config `htmx:configRequest`... The cleanest robust fix that directly maps to the verdict: \n\nOption A (JS abort on new request): keep a record of the last log request element; on new log request, abort the previous one. In htmx 2.x, you can send `htmx:abort` to an element to abort its in-flight request. On `htmx:beforeRequest` for log links, if `lastLogRequestElt` exists and differs, `htmx.trigger(lastLogRequestElt, 'htmx:abort')`. Then set `lastLogRequestElt = event.detail.elt`.\n\nOption B (discard stale responses): tag each request with the run id; in `htmx:beforeSwap` (or `htmx:oobBeforeSwap`), if the response's run id doesn't match the currently-open run (i.e., `lastTrigger`'s data), abort/discard.\n\nActually the most htmx-native approach is the hx-sync attribute. Let's check which htmx version is vendored. The verdict mentioned \"vendored HTMX 2.0.9\" in another verdict, so htmx 2.x. hx-sync exists in htmx 2.0.\n\nLet me think about using hx-sync with a shared target element. If each link has `hx-sync=\"#log-drawer-body:replace\"`, then: when link B issues a request, htmx looks for in-flight requests among elements that match `#log-drawer-body`? No — the sele