(e){const t=e.detail.elt||e.target;const n=oe(t);if(n&&n.xhr){n.xhr.abort()}});const n=window.onpopstate?window.onpopstate.bind(window):null;window.onpopstate=function(e){if(e.state&&e.state.htmx){en();ie(t,function(e){ae(e,\"htmx:restored\",{document:te(),triggerEvent:ae})})}else{if(n){n(e) ...\n====\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788550170975},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I see the abort handler:\n\n```js\nbody.addEventListener(\"htmx:abort\", function(e) {\n  const t = e.detail.elt || e.target;\n  const n = getInternalData(t);\n  if (n && n.xhr) { n.xhr.abort(); }\n});\n```\n\nIt's a delegated listener on `document.body` (well, `te().body`). So `htmx:abort` events bubble from whichever element they're dispatched to up to the body, and `e.detail.elt || e.target` identifies the target element. So when `replace` strategy fires `ae(h, \"htmx:abort\")` = `triggerEvent(h, \"htmx:abort\")` on element `h`, the delegated handler on body finds `t = h`, looks up `getInternalData(h).xhr`, and aborts it. But wait — is the in-flight xhr stored in `getInternalData(h)`? Let's trace: in `issueRequest` (the big function), `u = getInternalData(h)` where `h` is the sync group element; then later `const g = new XMLHttpRequest; u.xhr = g; u.abortable = B;` — so the xhr is stored on `getInternalData(h)`, the sync group element.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. When the request completes: `const m = function() { u.xhr = null; ... }` — hmm, `u.xhr = null` on the sync element. And when the request is issued from a normal (non-sync) element... Let me re-check: in the code path, `u` is defined somewhere before as `oe(r)` (getInternalData(elt)) and then reassigned to `oe(h)` in the sync case. Then `u.xhr = g` is set at the end after creating the XHR. So the xhr is always stored on `getInternalData(h)` where `h` is the sync target (or `r` if no sync). OK.\n\nSo `hx-sync=\"this:replace\"` on a shared ancestor wrapper: \n1. Link B clicked while A's request in flight: `h` = ancestor (closest with hx-sync), `u = getInternalData(ancestor)`, `u.xhr` = A's xhr. Strategy `replace` → `triggerEvent(ancestor, \"htmx:abort\")` → bubbles to body → `t = ancestor`, `getInternalData(ancestor).xhr.abort()`. Good, A aborted.\n2. Then... wait, after aborting, does the `if (u.xhr)` block run? After the `replace` branch, `ae(h,\"htmx:abort\")` fires synchronously. Then `if (u.xhr) { if (u.abortable) { ae(h, \"htmx:abort\") } else { queue... } }`. Hmm, when `xhr.abort()` is called synchronously, the `m` cleanup function sets `u.xhr = null`... Actually `xhr.abort()` fires events; the cleanup (`u.xhr = null`) may be synchronous or asynchronous (onreadystatechange / onloadend). The inner `if (u.xhr)` re-check: if `u.xhr` is still set after abort (not yet cleaned), `u.abortable` is true (B was set... wait no — B is set only in the `abort` strategy case; in this case B=false). Hmm, wait: for `replace` strategy, `B` (abortable) remains false at that point.\n\nLooking at the flow: if `u.xhr` still non-null after `ae(h,\"htmx:abort\")`:\n```js\nif (u.xhr) {\n  if (u.abortable) { ae(h, \"htmx:abort\"); }\n  else {\n    ...queue the request...\n    re(s); return false;\n  }\n}\n```\nWait — hmm, that would queue the new request rather than issue it immediately. Actually, this is the intended behavior of the `replace` strategy in htmx — the new request is queued (default \"last\") after the abort. Hmm, but with `replace`, docs say the current request is aborted and replaced by the new one. The queuing happens because after abort, a new request starts once the old one finishes cleaning up. Actually looking at the code: if `u.xhr` is null (abort cleaned synchronously), it falls through and issues the new request immediately. If `u.xhr` isn't null yet, it queues the new request with default \"last\" strategy and returns false. Then when the old request completes/aborts, `m` runs and processes the queued request.\n\nEither way, the outcome: A's request is aborted, B's request proceeds (possibly queued briefly). This is exactly what the verdict asks: \"request serialization/abort\".\n\nBut hold on — I need a wrapper element that all log links share. The log links are in `instance.html` rows. `instance.html` is embedded into `page.html` via `{{template \"instance.html\" .Selected}}`. The links are in tables for LiveRuns and RecentRuns. There are two separate tables. A common ancestor for both tables... let me look at the full structure of instance.html and page.html.\n\nActually, an alternative approach that doesn't depend on hx-sync subtleties is the JS event-based approach in canopy.js, which is also more testable:\n\nIn canopy.js:\n1. Track `lastLogRequest` element.\n2. On `htmx:beforeRequest` where `event.detail.elt` has `[data-log-open]` (or target is `#log-drawer-body`): if there's a previous log request element different from the current one, abort it via `htmx.trigger(prev, \"htmx:abort\")`. Hmm wait — but \"htmx:abort\" needs to target the element whose xhr is tracked: `getInternalData(elt).xhr`. The xhr is stored on the elt that issued the request (no hx-sync involved). Actually with the delegated abort handler: `const t = e.detail.elt || e.target; const n = getInternalData(t); if (n && n.xhr) n.xhr.abort();`. So dispatch `htmx:abort` on the requesting element — `getInternalData(elt).xhr` will be found there. \n\nWait, is the xhr stored on the requesting element (the link)? Without hx-sync, `h = r` (the elt itself), so `u = getInternalData(r)` and `u.xhr = g`. Yes. So `htmx.trigger(previousLink, \"htmx:abort\")` aborts the previous in-flight request. And `openDrawer` already shows \"Loading log…\" placeholder on open, which handles the UI.\n\nBut there's a second aspect to the race: even with abort on new request, the old request might already be in the \"responses arrived\" phase or the abort arrives after the response completed but before the swap. The swap happens via htmx's swap machinery. There's a `htmx:beforeSwap` event where we could check if the response is stale relative to the currently-open run and cancel the swap.\n\nActually, a cleaner and fully robust approach that doesn't rely on timing races between abort and swap: tag each request with its run (the drawer's open state) and discard stale responses at beforeSwap. But the verdict says \"Add request serialization/abort **or** discard stale responses before swap.\" Either approach satisfies.\n\nLet me think about which single approach is simplest and verifiably closes the race:\n\nApproach 1 (hx-sync on the drawer/canvas): Add a wrapping element... The links are directly inside table cells (`<td>`), in two different tables. Adding hx-sync to a shared ancestor: `instance.html`'s root is likely `.instance-detail` or similar. Let me check the structure. A wrapper `<tbody hx-sync=\"this:replace\">`? No — the two tables are separate; each tbody could have `hx-sync=\"this:replace\"` but that only serializes per-table. The race spans both tables (LiveRuns and RecentRuns, and possibly a future detail). Actually the drawer is shared; the sync scope should be global across all log links. Could put `hx-sync=\"this:replace\"` on the `<main id=\"main-content\">` element in page.html — all log links are descendants of main. Then all log-link requests sync against main's internal data. That's a one-attribute change... but wait, main also contains other hx-get links (selection links etc.). If those issue requests while a log request is in flight, they'd abort the log request (since the group key is main). Hmm — is that a problem? Selection links hx-get to swap main content — if you click a selection while a log loads, the drawer would show \"Loading\" — actually openDrawer sets the loading placeholder; if the log request gets aborted by a selection request, the drawer shows loading forever... Also the log request would abort a selection request. That's undesirable cross-feature coupling.\n\nBetter: scope hx-sync to only the log links. Since hx-sync's `this` form uses `closest(elt, \"hx-sync\")` — the nearest ancestor with the attribute — I could add the attribute to each log link itself: `hx-sync=\"this:replace\"` on each link. But then the group is keyed per-link (`h = closest(link, \"hx-sync\")` = the link itself), so A's request and B's request are separate groups; no cross-link sync. Doesn't work.\n\nWhat about a common wrapper shared by all log links but by no other hx-get elements? The drawer is in page.html. The log links are in the tables. There's no natural common element except main. Hmm.\n\nOK here's the thing — a dedicated JS guard in canopy.js is cleaner:\n\n```js\nvar logRequest = null;  // element with an in-flight log request\n\nfunction cancelLogRequest() {\n  if (logRequest && typeof htmx !== \"undefined\") {\n    htmx.trigger(logRequest, \"htmx:abort\");\n  }\n  logRequest = null;\n}\n\ndocument.addEventListener(\"htmx:beforeRequest\", function (event) {\n  var elt = event.detail.elt;\n  if (!(elt && elt.closest && elt.closest(\"[data-log-open]\"))) {\n    return;\n  }\n  if (logRequest && logRequest !== elt) {\n    htmx.trigger(logRequest, \"htmx:abort\");   // abort stale A\n  }\n  logRequest = elt;\n]);\n```\n\nWait — but the issue: the aborted request A's response may still swap if the abort raced with a completed response. If A's response already completed (xhr done) and is in the swap phase, `getInternalData(A).xhr.abort()` won't stop the swap. But actually if A's response completed and was swapped already, then B was opened after (openDrawer resets content to \"Loading…\"), and then A's swap would happen after B's open... Race window: B clicked → beforeRequest → abort A (xhr.abort() fails to stop if A is already fully loaded and being swapped, or if A's readystatechange already queued the swap).\n\nTo be thorough, add a stale-response guard at `htmx:beforeSwap`:\n\n```js\ndocument.addEventListener(\"htmx:beforeSwap\", function (event) {\n  var elt = event.detail.elt;\n  if (!(elt && elt.closest && elt.closest(\"[data-log-open]\"))) return;\n  // Only the most recent log request may swap into the drawer.\n  if (logRequest !== elt) {\n    event.preventDefault();   // discard stale response\n  }\n});\n```\n\nHmm — but `htmx:beforeSwap`'s `event.detail` has `shouldSwap` etc. `preventDefault` on beforeSwap cancels the swap. Let me verify the vendored htmx behavior for `htmx:beforeSwap`: \n\nFrom htmx docs: `htmx:beforeSwap` — \"This event is triggered before a swap is done... if you call preventDefault() the swap will not occur.\" Let me verify in the vendored minified copy.\n\nAlso there's the afterRequest cleanup: when a log request completes, set `logRequest = null` — but only if it's still the current one. Use `htmx:afterRequest` (or `htmx:afterSwap`) to clear `logRequest` if it matches elt.\n\nActually, careful: `htmx:afterRequest` fires for both success and error and abort? Let me check the vendored htmx events. There's `htmx:afterRequest` fired in `m` cleanup function (on load end). Let me verify.\n\nAlternatively simpler robust mechanism: put the \"request id\" on the link as a dataset and compare in beforeSwap against `lastTrigger`:\n\n```js\ndocument.addEventListener(\"htmx:beforeSwap\", function (event) {\n  var elt = event.detail.elt;\n  if (!(elt && elt.closest && elt.closest(\"[data-log-open]\"))) return;\n  var panel = drawer();\n  var active = lastTrigger && document.contains(lastTrigger) ? lastTrigger : null;\n  if (active !== elt) event.preventDefault();\n});\n```\n\n`lastTrigger` is set in `openDrawer(trigger)` — the link that was last clicked. So at swap time, if the responding link is not the last-opened one, the swap is stale and gets discarded.\n\nCombined approach:\n1. **Abort on new request** (serialization): in `htmx:beforeRequest` (or on the click handler before opening), abort any in-flight previous log request.\n2. **Discard stale swaps**: in `htmx:beforeSwap`, cancel the swap if the request element isn't the current `lastTrigger`.\n\nActually, simpler: do both in the click handler and the swap guard. Let me also consider: the \"open\" click handler in canopy.js already intercepts clicks with no modifier and calls `openDrawer(openLink)`. Note: clicking the link ALSO triggers htmx (the hx-get) — the click handler doesn't preventDefault, so the browser follows the href and htmx issues the request. Wait: htmx intercepts the click on the anchor with hx-get and prevents default. Our click listener runs first (registration order: canopy.js adds listener on `document` — htmx also listens on document for clicks). Both will fire; htmx's handler will issue the AJAX request. Actually if our handler doesn't preventDefault, that's fine.\n\nImportant: with a modifier key (meta/ctrl/shift), we skip openDrawer but htmx still performs hx-get? Hmm — htmx by default also respects modifier keys? Actually htmx 2.x by default ignores modified clicks? Not necessarily... hx-boost does. Plain hx-get on an anchor — htmx will handle the click regardless of modifiers unless configured... Not critical for this fix; the original behavior.\n\nNow — where to abort: in the click handler right when opening a new drawer with a different trigger, abort the previous in-flight request:\n\n```js\nif (openLink) {\n  if (unmodified click) {\n    if (lastTrigger && lastTrigger !== openLink && lastLogElt...) abort\n    openDrawer(openLink);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBut the request element aborted must be the one whose xhr is tracked: `getInternalData(elt).xhr`. Since without hx-sync the xhr is stored on the link itself (`u = oe(r)`, `u.xhr = g`), we need the link that issued the request. So track `lastLogRequestElt` on beforeRequest, or use `lastTrigger` (the link clicked = the link that issues the request). `lastTrigger` is exactly the link. But after closeDrawer, lastTrigger is null — the in-flight request... if the drawer is closed, the request still proceeds (htmx will still swap into #log-drawer-body even if hidden). Should closing the drawer abort the in-flight request? Not required by the verdict. Keep minimal.\n\nRace hazard with using `lastTrigger` for abort: `htmx:abort` dispatched to `lastTrigger` — if that element's xhr already completed, `getInternalData(lastTrigger).xhr` — is it null after completion? In the cleanup `m`, `u.xhr = null` — u is `getInternalData(h)` = `getInternalData(link)` (no hx-sync). Yes, after completion xhr is null, so abort is a no-op. Good.\n\nAnd the stale-swap guard uses `lastTrigger` identity: at beforeSwap time, if `lastTrigger !== elt` (the element that issued the request), preventDefault. Given `openDrawer` sets lastTrigger = openLink for the latest click, and the swap for the latest request elt === lastTrigger, correct swaps proceed; stale ones (A arriving after B's open) get discarded.\n\nBut wait — there's an ordering subtlety: When does htmx fire the request relative to our click handler and openDrawer? Both are `click` listeners on document. Registration order: htmx.min.js is loaded before canopy.js (defer order — `<script src=\"/static/htmx.min.js\" defer></script>` then `<script src=\"/static/canopy.js\" defer></script>`), so htmx registers first? Actually htmx registers its click handler on `document.body`/document during init (on DOMContentLoaded / immediately since script is at end of body... with `defer`, it executes after parsing, in order: htmx first, then canopy.js). htmx listens on `body` for clicks (event delegation). canopy.js adds a listener on `document`. A click on the link bubbles: link → ... → body (htmx handler fires → issues request) → document (canopy handler fires → openDrawer). Hmm, that means htmx issues the request BEFORE openDrawer sets lastTrigger. But our abort logic is in the click handler (openDrawer), and the swap guard checks lastTrigger at swap time — well after. So ordering at click time doesn't matter for correctness of the swap guard... as long as openDrawer sets lastTrigger before any swap happens (it does; swap happens at response time).\n\nHmm wait, but there's one more subtle thing: with the request issued before openDrawer runs, if the user clicks link B while A is in flight: click on B → body handler: htmx issues B's request. Document handler: openDrawer(B) — this is where we abort A if A is still in flight. But B's request was already issued. Ordering: hit A's abort before B's response arrives — fine, B's response arrives later and swaps.\n\nBut there's a potential in-between race: B's request issued → openDrawer(B) aborts A → A aborted (no swap) → B returns → beforeSwap: lastTrigger === B === elt → swap. \n\nAnother path: A returns just before B's click: A's swap fires with lastTrigger === A (since B not clicked yet) → fine, A's log is shown. Then B clicked → new request → B's response swaps. That's correct sequential behavior.\n\nPath: A returns AFTER B's click but BEFORE our openDrawer handler runs? Click on B: body handler (htmx) issues B request — but wait, htmx issues synchronously during the click event at body level; then document-level handler (canopy) runs openDrawer(B). Can A's swap happen between body handler and document handler during the same click event? No — JS is single-threaded; A's response handling (readystatechange → swap) is a separate task queued on the event loop; it can't interleave within the synchronous click dispatch. The click event propagates synchronously through all listeners. So no interleaving within a click.\n\nWhat about A's swap occurring between B's request issue and... no, again single threaded: once B's click handler completes, lastTrigger = B (set in openDrawer). Any subsequent swap for A will be checked against lastTrigger=B → discarded. \n\nSo the fix:\n\nIn canopy.js:\n- When opening the drawer for a different link, abort the previous in-flight log request (if any).\n- Guard `htmx:beforeSwap` — actually wait, one more consideration: the guard should also handle the case where the drawer is closed (lastTrigger null) — a stale response arriving after close would swap into the hidden drawer body... The current closeDrawer clears content and hides. If a late response arrives, it would swap content into the hidden drawer — which is harmless-ish but would fill the drawer with a stale log that reappears when opened. Hmm — actually if lastTrigger is null at swap time, with my guard `active !== elt` → preventDefault → discard. That's an improvement, and it also covers \"drawer closed while request in flight\".\n\nBut hold on — should closing the drawer abort the request? The verdict focuses on the cross-run overwrite race; discarding stale swaps covers it. Keep the change minimal: add abort-on-new-open + stale-swap guard.\n\nNow let me consider testability. The repo has server_test.go. Tests for templates exist (server_test.go checks rendered HTML contains `data-log-open`, `hx-target=\"#log-drawer-body\"`, etc.). A regression test: how do we test the JS race? There's no JS test harness. The verdict is about behavior in static/canopy.js. server_test.go is Go. There's templates_test.go per other verdicts. Let me look at how tests are structured to see what testable surface exists.\n\nWhat observable defect can we regression-test? The hx-sync attribute approach would be testable via template rendering (assert `hx-sync` present in the rendered link). But for the JS approach, the template contains only `data-log-open` links — no testable marker in the DOM.\n\nHmm — maybe I should blend: add `hx-sync=\"#log-drawer:replace\"`? Wait, hx-sync selector must resolve via `closest(elt, selector)` to a common ancestor — log links are not within `#log-drawer`. So that doesn't work.\n\nWhat about targeting the whole document? `hx-sync=\"body:replace\"`? `closest(link, \"body\")` = body — that groups ALL requests in the document against body — again cross-feature coupling (selection swaps would abort log loads). Not good.\n\nWhat about `hx-sync=\"main:replace\"`? Same cross-feature issue.\n\nCould put a dedicated wrapper around just the log links... The links live in two separate tables under main. Wrapping the drawer target? No.\n\nAlternatively, per-table `hx-sync`: Add `hx-sync=\"this:replace\"` on each `<tbody>`? The race is between runs in LiveRuns table and runs in RecentRuns table... Actually — realistically the race is any two log links. Per-table sync reduces but doesn't eliminate the cross-table race. Not sufficient.\n\nBetter: use `hx-sync` with `closest` — hmm, the selector is matched with `closest(elt, selector)`, so any ancestor selector works. There's no natural ancestor limited to log links.\n\nOK so the JS route is the clean one. For testability, I can add a small regression test in Go that renders instance.html and asserts the log link carries a stable marker showing the sync guard applies — e.g., assert `class=\"log-link\"` already exists... Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe restructure so the templates include the run id in a data attribute on the link — giving the JS the identity it needs without relying on `lastTrigger` element identity, AND giving tests a surface. E.g., the swap guard could read `data-log-open`... but the run id isn't strictly needed if we track elt identity.\n\nLet me look at server_test.go to see existing tests around the log drawer and how they render.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Now I see the abort handler:\\n\\n```js\\nbody.addEventListener(\\\"htmx:abort\\\", function(e) {\\n  const t = e.detail.elt || e.target;\\n  const n = getInternalData(t);\\n  if (n && n.xhr) { n.xhr.abort(); }\\n});\\n```\\n\\nIt's a delegated listener on `document.body` (well, `te().body`). So `htmx:abort` events bubble from whichever element they're dispatched to up to the body, and `e.detail.elt || e.target` identifies the target element. So when `replace` strategy fires `ae(h, \\\"htmx:abort\\\")` = `triggerEvent(h, \\\"htmx:abort\\\")` on element `h`, the delegated handler on body finds `t = h`, looks up `getInternalData(h).xhr`, and aborts it. But wait — is the in-flight xhr stored in `getInternalData(h)`? Let's trace: in `issueRequest` (the big function), `u = getInternalData(h)` where `h` is the sync group element; then later `const g = new XMLHttpRequest; u.xhr = g; u.abortable = B;` — so the xhr is stored on `getInternalData(h)`, the sync group element.\\n\\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. When the request completes: `const m = function() { u.xhr = null; ... }` — hmm, `u.xhr = null` on the sync element. And when the request is issued from a normal (non-sync) element... Let me re-check: in the code path, `u` is defined somewhere before as `oe(r)` (getInternalData(elt)) and then reassigned to `oe(h)` in the sync case. Then `u.xhr = g` is set at the end after creating the XHR. So the xhr is always stored on `getInternalData(h)` where `h` is the sync target (or `r` if no sync). OK.\\n\\nSo `hx-sync=\\\"this:replace\\\"` on a shared ancestor wrapper: \\n1. Link B clicked while A's request in flight: `h` = ancestor (closest with hx-sync), `u = getInternalData(ancestor)`, `u.xhr` = A's xhr. Strategy `replace` → `triggerEvent(ancestor, \\\"htmx:abort\\\")` → bubbles to body → `t = ancestor`, `getInternalData(ancestor).xhr.abort()`. Good, A aborted.\\n2. Then... wait, after aborting, does the `if (u.xhr)` block run? After the `replace` branch, `ae(h,\\\"htmx:abort\\\")` fires synchronously. Then `if (u.xhr) { if (u.abortable) { ae(h, \\\"htmx:abort\\\") } else { queue... } }`. Hmm, when `xhr.abort()` is called synchronously, the `m` cleanup function sets `u.xhr = null`... Actually `xhr.abort()` fires events; the cleanup (`u.xhr = null`) may be synchronous or asynchronous (onreadystatechange / onloadend). The inner `if (u.xhr)` re-check: if `u.xhr` is still set after abort (not yet cleaned), `u.abortable` is true (B was set... wait no — B is set only in the `abort` strategy case; in this case B=false). Hmm, wait: for `replace` strategy, `B` (abortable) remains false at that point.\\n\\nLooking at the flow: if `u.xhr` still non-null after `ae(h,\\\"htmx:abort\\\")`:\\n```js\\nif (u.xhr) {\\n  if (u.abortable) { ae(h, \\\"htmx:abort\\\"); }\\n  else {\\n    ...queue the request...\\n    re(s); return false;\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\nWait — hmm, that would queue the new request rather than issue it immediately. Actually, this is the intended behavior of the `replace` strategy in htmx — the new request is queued (default \\\"last\\\") after the abort. Hmm, but with `replace`, docs say the current request is aborted and replaced by the new one. The queuing happens because after abort, a new request starts once the old one finishes cleaning up. Actually looking at the code: if `u.xhr` is null (abort cleaned synchronously), it falls through and issues the new request immediately. If `u.xhr` isn't null yet, it queues the new request with default \\\"last\\\" strategy and returns false. Then when the old request completes/aborts, `m` runs and processes the queued request.\\n\\nEither way, the outcome: A's request is aborted, B's request proceeds (possibly queued briefly). This is exactly what the verdict asks: \\\"request serialization/abort\\\".\\n\\nBut hold on — I need a wrapper element that all log links share. The log links are in `instance.html` rows. `instance.html` is embedded into `page.html` via `{{template \\\"instance.html\\\" .Selected}}`. The links are in tables for LiveRuns and RecentRuns. There are two separate tables. A common ancestor for both tables... let me look at the full structure of instance.html and page.html.\\n\\nActually, an alternative approach that doesn't depend on hx-sync subtleties is the JS event-based approach in canopy.js, which is also more testable:\\n\\nIn canopy.js:\\n1. Track `lastLogRequest` element.\\n2. On `htmx:beforeRequest` where `event.detail.elt` has `[data-log-open]` (or target is `#log-drawer-body`): if there's a previous log request element different from the current one, abort it via `htmx.trigger(prev, \\\"htmx:abort\\\")`. Hmm wait — but \\\"htmx:abort\\\" needs to target the element whose xhr is tracked: `getInternalData(elt).xhr`. The xhr is stored on the elt that issued the request (no hx-sync involved). Actually with the delegated abort handler: `const t = e.detail.elt || e.target; const n = getInternalData(t); if (n && n.xhr) n.xhr.abort();`. So dispatch `htmx:abort` on the requesting element — `getInternalData(elt).xhr` will be found there. \\n\\nWait, is the xhr stored on the requesting element (the link)? Without hx-sync, `h = r` (the elt itself), so `u = getInternalData(r)` and `u.xhr = g`. Yes. So `htmx.trigger(previousLink, \\\"htmx:abort\\\")` aborts the previous in-flight request. And `openDrawer` already shows \\\"Loading log…\\\" placeholder on open, which handles the UI.\\n\\nBut there's a second aspect to the race: even with abort on new request, the old request might already be in the \\\"responses arrived\\\" phase or the abort arrives after the response completed but before the swap. The swap happens via htmx's swap machinery. There's a `htmx:beforeSwap` event where we could check if the response is stale relative to the currently-open run and cancel the swap.\\n\\nActually, a cleaner and fully robust approach that doesn't rely on timing races between abort and swap: tag each request with its run (the drawer's open state) and discard stale responses at beforeSwap. But the verdict says \\\"Add request serialization/abort **or** discard stale responses before swap.\\\" Either approach satisfies.\\n\\nLet me think about which single approach is simplest and verifiably closes the race:\\n\\nApproach 1 (hx-sync on the drawer/canvas): Add a wrapping element... The links are directly inside table cells (`<td>`), in two different tables. Adding hx-sync to a shared ancestor: `instance.html`'s root is likely `.instance-detail` or similar. Let me check the structure. A wrapper `<tbody hx-sync=\\\"this:replace\\\">`? No — the two tables are separate; each tbody could have `hx-sync=\\\"this:replace\\\"` but that only serializes per-table. The race spans both tables (LiveRuns and RecentRuns, and possibly a future detail). Actually the drawer is shared; the sync scope should be global across all log links. Could put `hx-sync=\\\"this:replace\\\"` on the `<main id=\\\"main-content\\\">` element in page.html — all log links are descendants of main. Then all log-link requests sync against main's internal data. That's a one-attribute change... but wait, main also contains other hx-get links (selection links etc.). If those issue requests while a log request is in flight, they'd abort the log request (since the group key is main). Hmm — is that a problem? Selection links hx-get to swap main content — if you click a selection while a log loads, the drawer would show \\\"Loading\\\" — actually openDrawer sets the loading placeholder; if the log request gets aborted by a selection request, the drawer shows loading forever... Also the log request would abort a selection request. That's undesirable cross-feature coupling.\\n\\nBetter: scope hx-sync to only the log links. Since hx-sync's `this` form uses `closest(elt, \\\"hx-sync\\\")` — the nearest ancestor with the attribute — I could add the attribute to each log link itself: `hx-sync=\\\"this:replace\\\"` on each link. But then the group is keyed per-link (`h = closest(link, \\\"hx-sync\\\")` = the link itself), so A's request and B's request are separate groups; no cross-link sync. Doesn't work.\\n\\nWhat about a common wrapper shared by all log links but by no other hx-get elements? The drawer is in page.html. The log links are in the tables. There's no natural common element except main. Hmm.\\n\\nOK here's the thing — a dedicated JS guard in canopy.js is cleaner:\\n\\n```js\\nvar logRequest = null;  // element with an in-flight log request\\n\\nfunction cancelLogRequest() {\\n  if (logRequest && typeof htmx !== \\\"undefined\\\") {\\n    htmx.trigger(logRequest, \\\"htmx:abort\\\");\\n  }\\n  logRequest = null;\\n}\\n\\ndocument.addEventListener(\\\"htmx:beforeRequest\\\", function (event) {\\n  var elt = event.detail.elt;\\n  if (!(elt && elt.closest && elt.closest(\\\"[data-log-open]\\\"))) {\\n    return;\\n  }\\n  if (logRequest && logRequest !== elt) {\\n    htmx.trigger(logRequest, \\\"htmx:abort\\\");   // abort stale A\\n  }\\n  logRequest = elt;\\n]);\\n```\\n\\nWait — but the issue: the aborted request A's response may still swap if the abort raced with a completed response. If A's response already completed (xhr done) and is in the swap phase, `getInternalData(A).xhr.abort()` won't stop the swap. But actually if A's response completed and was swapped already, then B was opened after (openDrawer resets content to \\\"Loading…\\\"), and then A's swap would happen after B's open... Race window: B clicked → beforeRequest → abort A (xhr.abort() fails to stop if A is already fully loaded and being swapped, or if A's readystatechange already queued the swap).\\n\\nTo be thorough, add a stale-response guard at `htmx:beforeSwap`:\\n\\n```js\\ndocument.addEventListener(\\\"htmx:beforeSwap\\\", function (event) {\\n  var elt = event.detail.elt;\\n  if (!(elt && elt.closest && elt.closest(\\\"[data-log-open]\\\"))) return;\\n  // Only the most recent log request may swap into the drawer.\\n  if (logRequest !== elt) {\\n    event.preventDefault();   // discard stale response\\n  }\\n});\\n```\\n\\nHmm — but `htmx:beforeSwap`'s `event.detail` has `shouldSwap` etc. `preventDefault` on beforeSwap cancels the swap. Let me verify the vendored htmx behavior for `htmx:beforeSwap`: \\n\\nFrom htmx docs: `htmx:beforeSwap` — \\\"This event is triggered before a swap is done... if you call preventDefault() the swap will not occur.\\\" Let me verify in the vendored minified copy.\\n\\nAlso there's the afterRequest cleanup: when a log request completes, set `logRequest = null` — but only if it's still the current one. Use `htmx:afterRequest` (or `htmx:afterSwap`) to clear `logRequest` if it matches elt.\\n\\nActually, careful: `htmx:afterRequest` fires for both success and error and abort? Let me check the vendored htmx events. There's `htmx:afterRequest` fired in `m` cleanup function (on load end). Let me verify.\\n\\nAlternatively simpler robust mechanism: put the \\\"request id\\\" on the link as a dataset and compare in beforeSwap against `lastTrigger`:\\n\\n```js\\ndocument.addEventListener(\\\"htmx:beforeSwap\\\", function (event) {\\n  var elt = event.detail.elt;\\n  if (!(elt && elt.closest && elt.closest(\\\"[data-log-open]\\\"))) return;\\n  var panel = drawer();\\n  var active = lastTrigger && document.contains(lastTrigger) ? lastTrigger : null;\\n  if (active !== elt) event.preventDefault();\\n});\\n```\\n\\n`lastTrigger` is set in `openDrawer(trigger)` — the link that was last clicked. So at swap time, if the responding link is not the last-opened one, the swap is stale and gets discarded.\\n\\nCombined approach:\\n1. **Abort on new request** (serialization): in `htmx:beforeRequest` (or on the click handler before opening), abort any in-flight previous log request.\\n2. **Discard stale swaps**: in `htmx:beforeSwap`, cancel the swap if the request element isn't the current `lastTrigger`.\\n\\nActually, simpler: do both in the click handler and the swap guard. Let me also consider: the \\\"open\\\" click handler in canopy.js already intercepts clicks with no modifier and calls `openDrawer(openLink)`. Note: clicking the link ALSO triggers htmx (the hx-get) — the click handler doesn't preventDefault, so the browser follows the href and htmx issues the request. Wait: htmx intercepts the click on the anchor with hx-get and prevents default. Our click listener runs first (registration order: canopy.js adds listener on `document` — htmx also listens on document for clicks). Both will fire; htmx's handler will issue the AJAX request. Actually if our handler doesn't preventDefault, that's fine.\\n\\nImportant: with a modifier key (meta/ctrl/shift), we skip openDrawer but htmx still performs hx-get? Hmm — htmx by default also respects modifier keys? Actually htmx 2.x by default ignores modified clicks? Not necessarily... hx-boost does. Plain hx-get on an anchor — htmx will handle the click regardless of modifiers unless configured... Not critical for this fix; the original behavior.\\n\\nNow — where to abort: in the click handler right when opening a new drawer with a different trigger, abort the previous in-flight request:\\n\\n```js\\nif (openLink) {\\n  if (unmodified click) {\\n    if (lastTrigger && lastTrigger !== openLink && lastLogElt...) abort\\n    openDrawer(openLink);\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nBut the request element aborted must be the one whose xhr is tracked: `getInternalData(elt).xhr`. Since without hx-sync the xhr is stored on the link itself (`u = oe(r)`, `u.xhr = g`), we need the link that issued the request. So track `lastLogRequestElt` on beforeRequest, or use `lastTrigger` (the link clicked = the link that issues the request). `lastTrigger` is exactly the link. But after closeDrawer, lastTrigger is null — the in-flight request... if the drawer is closed, the request still proceeds (htmx will still swap into #log-drawer-body even if hidden). Should closing the drawer abort the in-flight request? Not required by the verdict. Keep minimal.\\n\\nRace hazard with using `lastTrigger` for abort: `htmx:abort` dispatched to `lastTrigger` — if that element's xhr already completed, `getInternalData(lastTrigger).xhr` — is it null after completion? In the cleanup `m`, `u.xhr = null` — u is `getInternalData(h)` = `getInternalData(link)` (no hx-sync). Yes, after completion xhr is null, so abort is a no-op. Good.\\n\\nAnd the stale-swap guard uses `lastTrigger` identity: at beforeSwap time, if `lastTrigger !== elt` (the element that issued the request), preventDefault. Given `openDrawer` sets lastTrigger = openLink for the latest click, and the swap for the latest request elt === lastTrigger, correct swaps proceed; stale ones (A arriving after B's open) get discarded.\\n\\nBut wait — there's an ordering subtlety: When does htmx fire the request relative to our click handler and openDrawer? Both are `click` listeners on document. Registration order: htmx.min.js is loaded before canopy.js (defer order — `<script src=\\\"/static/htmx.min.js\\\" defer></script>` then `<script src=\\\"/static/canopy.js\\\" defer></script>`), so htmx registers first? Actually htmx registers its click handler on `document.body`/document during init (on DOMContentLoaded / immediately since script is at end of body... with `defer`, it executes after parsing, in order: htmx first, then canopy.js). htmx listens on `body` for clicks (event delegation). canopy.js adds a listener on `document`. A click on the link bubbles: link → ... → body (htmx handler fires → issues request) → document (canopy handler fires → openDrawer). Hmm, that means htmx issues the request BEFORE openDrawer sets lastTrigger. But our abort logic is in the click handler (openDrawer), and the swap guard checks lastTrigger at swap time — well after. So ordering at click time doesn't matter for correctness of the swap guard... as long as openDrawer sets lastTrigger before any swap happens (it does; swap happens at response time).\\n\\nHmm wait, but there's one more subtle thing: with the request issued before openDrawer runs, if the user clicks link B while A is in flight: click on B → body handler: htmx issues B's request. Document handler: openDrawer(B) — this is where we abort A if A is still in flight. But B's request was already issued. Ordering: hit A's abort before B's response arrives — fine, B's response arrives later and swaps.\\n\\nBut there's a potential in-between race: B's request issued → openDrawer(B) aborts A → A aborted (no swap) → B returns → beforeSwap: lastTrigger === B === elt → swap. \\n\\nAnother path: A returns just before B's click: A's swap fires with lastTrigger === A (since B not clicked yet) → fine, A's log is shown. Then B clicked → new request → B's response swaps. That's correct sequential behavior.\\n\\nPath: A returns AFTER B's click but BEFORE our openDrawer handler runs? Click on B: body handler (htmx) issues B request — but wait, htmx issues synchronously during the click event at body level; then document-level handler (canopy) runs openDrawer(B). Can A's swap happen between body handler and document handler during the same click event? No — JS is single-threaded; A's response handling (readystatechange → swap) is a separate task queued on the event loop; it can't interleave within the synchronous click dispatch. The click event propagates synchronously through all listeners. So no interleaving within a click.\\n\\nWhat about A's swap occurring between B's request issue and... no, again single threaded: once B's click handler completes, lastTrigger = B (set in openDrawer). Any subsequent swap for A will be checked against lastTrigger=B → discarded. \\n\\nSo the fix:\\n\\nIn canopy.js:\\n- When opening the drawer for a different link, abort the previous in-flight log request (if any).\\n- Guard `htmx:beforeSwap` — actually wait, one more consideration: the guard should also handle the case where the drawer is closed (lastTrigger null) — a stale response arriving after close would swap into the hidden drawer body... The current closeDrawer clears content and hides. If a late response arrives, it would swap content into the hidden drawer — which is harmless-ish but would fill the drawer with a stale log that reappears when opened. Hmm — actually if lastTrigger is null at swap time, with my guard `active !== elt` → preventDefault → discard. That's an improvement, and it also covers \\\"drawer closed while request in flight\\\".\\n\\nBut hold on — should closing the drawer abort the request? The verdict focuses on the cross-run overwrite race; discarding stale swaps covers it. Keep the change minimal: add abort-on-new-open + stale-swap guard.\\n\\nNow let me consider testability. The repo has server_test.go. Tests for templates exist (server_test.go checks rendered HTML contains `data-log-open`, `hx-target=\\\"#log-drawer-body\\\"`, etc.). A regression test: how do we test the JS race? There's no JS test harness. The verdict is about behavior in static/canopy.js. server_test.go is Go. There's templates_test.go per other verdicts. Let me look at how tests are structured to see what testable surface exists.\\n\\nWhat observable defect can we regression-test? The hx-sync attribute approach would be testable via template rendering (assert `hx-sync` present in the rendered link). But for the JS approach, the template contains only `data-log-open` links — no testable marker in the DOM.\\n\\nHmm — maybe I should blend: add `hx-sync=\\\"#log-drawer:replace\\\"`? Wait, hx-sync selector must resolve via `closest(elt, selector)` to a common ancestor — log links are not within `#log-drawer`. So that doesn't work.\\n\\nWhat about targeting the whole document? `hx-sync=\\\"body:replace\\\"`? `closest(link, \\\"body\\\")` = body — that groups ALL requests in the document against body — again cross-feature coupling (selection swaps would abort log loads). Not good.\\n\\nWhat about `hx-sync=\\\"main:replace\\\"`? Same cross-feature issue.\\n\\nCould put a dedicated wrapper around just the log links... The links live in two separate tables under main. Wrapping the drawer target? No.\\n\\nAlternatively, per-table `hx-sync`: Add `hx-sync=\\\"this:replace\\\"` on each `<tbody>`? The race is between runs in LiveRuns table and runs in RecentRuns table... Actually — realistically the race is any two log links. Per-table sync reduces but doesn't eliminate the cross-table race. Not sufficient.\\n\\nBetter: use `hx-sync` with `closest` — hmm, the selector is matched with `closest(elt, selector)`, so any ancestor selector works. There's no natural ancestor limited to log links.\\n\\nOK so the JS route is the clean one. For testability, I can add a small regression test in Go that renders instance.html and asserts the log link carries a stable marker showing the sync guard applies — e.g., assert `class=\\\"log-link\\\"` already exists... Hmm.\\n\\nAlternatively, maybe restructure so the templates include the run id in a data attribute on the link — giving the JS the identity it needs without relying on `lastTrigger` element identity, AND giving tests a surface. E.g., the swap guard could read `data-log-open`... but the run id isn't strictly needed if we track elt identity.\\n\\nLet me look at server_test.go to see existing tests around the log drawer and how they render.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me look at the existing tests and the full template structure to understand the test surface."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ab28837e6f76437ebd135648","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/phaedrus/development/misty-step/canopy/.forest/worktrees/1788550107325557414-fixer/server_test.go"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731","usage":{"input":1313,"output":5365,"cacheRead":27136,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":5265,"totalTokens":33814,"cost":{"input":0.00006565000000000001,"output":0.0005365000000000001,"cacheRead":0.00027136,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0008735100000000001}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788550170976,"responseId":"gen-1788550170-AI9RNzi8L38TnzPN6mrE","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_ab28837e6f76437ebd135648","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"package main\n\nimport (\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"html/template\"\n\t\"net/http\"\n\t\"net/http/httptest\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"testing\"\n)\n\nfunc testTemplates(t *testing.T) *template.Template {\n\tt.Helper()\n\treturn template.Must(template.New(\"all\").Parse(`\n{{define \"page.html\"}}page{{end}}\n{{define \"fleet.html\"}}fleet{{end}}\n{{define \"instance.html\"}}instance{{end}}\n{{define \"log.html\"}}{{.State}}:{{.Error}}{{end}}\n`))\n}\n\nfunc TestHandlerRejectsUnknownInstance(t *testing.T) {\n\tapp := NewApp(testInventory(), &testCollector{}, testTemplates(t))\n\trecorder := httptest.NewRecorder()\n\tapp.Handler().ServeHTTP(recorder, httptest.NewRequest(http.MethodGet, \"/?instance=missing\", nil))\n\tif recorder.Code != http.StatusNotFound {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"status=%d, want %d\", recorder.Code, http.StatusNotFound)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestHandlerRejectsUnknownAndMalformedRun(t *testing.T) {\n\tcollector := &testCollector{}\n\tcollector.logs = func(_ context.Context, _ Instance, _ string, _ bool) (LogResult, error) {\n\t\treturn LogResult{}, &CLIError{Command: \"run logs\", Exit: 4, Message: \"not found\"}\n\t}\n\tapp := NewApp(testInventory(), collector, testTemplates(t))\n\tfor _, test := range []struct {\n\t\tname string\n\t\turl  string\n\t\tcode int\n\t}{\n\t\t{name: \"unknown\", url: \"/logs?instance=one&run=missing-run\", code: http.StatusOK},\n\t\t{name: \"malformed\", url: \"/logs?instance=one&run=bad%2Frun\", code: http.StatusBadRequest},\n\t\t{name: \"missing\", url: \"/logs?instance=one\", code: http.StatusBadRequest},\n\t} {\n\t\tt.Run(test.name, func(t *testing.T) {\n\t\t\trecorder := httptest.NewRecorder()\n\t\t\tapp.Handler().ServeHTTP(recorder, httptest.NewRequest(http.MethodGet, test.url, nil))\n\t\t\tif recorder.Code != test.code {\n\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"status=%d, want %d; body=%s\", recorder.Code, test.code, recorder.Body.String())\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif test.name == \"unknown\" && !strings.Contains(recorder.Body.String(), \"unknown\") {\n\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"body=%q, want unknown log state\", recorder.Body.String())\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t})\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestHandlerMapsEvictedLogToFragmentState(t *testing.T) {\n\tcollector := &testCollector{}\n\tcollector.logs = func(_ context.Context, _ Instance, runID string, _ bool) (LogResult, error) {\n\t\treturn LogResult{RunID: runID, Complete: true, Retained: false}, nil\n\t}\n\tapp := NewApp(testInventory(), collector, testTemplates(t))\n\trecorder := httptest.NewRecorder()\n\tapp.Handler().ServeHTTP(recorder, httptest.NewRequest(http.MethodGet, \"/logs?instance=one&run=old-run\", nil))\n\tif recorder.Code != http.StatusOK || !strings.Contains(recorder.Body.String(), \"evicted\") {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"status=%d body=%q, want evicted fragment\", recorder.Code, recorder.Body.String())\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestReadOnlyRoutesRejectMutationMethods(t *testing.T) {\n\tapp := NewApp(testInventory(), &testCollector{}, testTemplates(t))\n\tfor _, path := range []string{\"/\", \"/fragments/fleet\", \"/fragments/instance\", \"/logs?instance=one&run=run-1\", \"/healthz\", \"/static/canopy.css\"} {\n\t\trecorder := httptest.NewRecorder()\n\t\tapp.Handler().ServeHTTP(recorder, httptest.NewRequest(http.MethodPost, path, nil))\n\t\tif recorder.Code != http.StatusMethodNotAllowed {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"POST %s status=%d, want %d\", path, recorder.Code, http.StatusMethodNotAllowed)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestHandlerServesEmbeddedStaticFiles(t *testing.T) {\n\tapp := NewApp(testInventory(), &testCollector{}, testTemplates(t))\n\tfor _, path := range []string{\"/static/canopy.css\", \"/static/canopy.js\", \"/static/htmx.min.js\"} {\n\t\trecorder := httptest.NewRecorder()\n\t\tapp.Handler().ServeHTTP(recorder, httptest.NewRequest(http.MethodGet, path, nil))\n\t\tif recorder.Code != http.StatusOK || recorder.Body.Len() == 0 {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"GET %s status=%d bytes=%d, want non-empty 200\", path, recorder.Code, recorder.Body.Len())\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestInstanceSelectionTriggersFleetRefresh(t *testing.T) {\n\tinventory := testInventory()\n\tinventory.Instances = append(inventory.Instances, Instance{ID: \"two\", Label: \"Two\", Root: \"/tmp/two\", Forest: \"forest\"})\n\tapp := NewApp(inventory, &testCollector{}, testTemplates(t))\n\trecorder := httptest.NewRecorder()\n\tapp.Handler().ServeHTTP(recorder, httptest.NewRequest(http.MethodGet, \"/fragments/instance?instance=two\", nil))\n\tif recorder.Code != http.StatusOK {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"status=%d, want %d\", recorder.Code, http.StatusOK)\n\t}\n\tif got := recorder.Header().Get(\"HX-Trigger-After-Swap\"); got != \"canopy-selection\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"HX-Trigger-After-Swap=%q, want canopy-selection\", got)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestHealthIsProcessLivenessOnly(t *testing.T) {\n\tcollector := &testCollector{collect: func(_ context.Context, _ Instance) (Snapshot, error) {\n\t\treturn Snapshot{}, errors.New(\"offline\")\n\t}}\n\tapp := NewApp(testInventory(), collector, testTemplates(t))\n\trecorder := httptest.NewRecorder()\n\tapp.Handler().ServeHTTP(recorder, httptest.NewRequest(http.MethodGet, \"/healthz\", nil))\n\tif recorder.Code != http.StatusOK || recorder.Body.String() != \"ok\\n\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"health status=%d body=%q, want 200 ok\", recorder.Code, recorder.Body.String())\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestLogViewerTemplatesRenderDrawerAndCopyControl(t *testing.T) {\n\ttemplates, err := loadTemplates()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"load templates: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\n\tvar page bytes.Buffer\n\tif err := templates.ExecuteTemplate(&page, \"page.html\", PageView{}); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"render page template: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\tfor _, want := range []string{`id=\"log-drawer\"`, `id=\"log-drawer-body\"`, `data-log-close`, `/static/canopy.js`} {\n\t\tif !strings.Contains(page.String(), want) {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"page template missing %q\", want)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tselected := InstanceView{\n\t\tID:         \"one\",\n\t\tFreshness:  \"fresh\",\n\t\tLiveRuns:   []LiveRunViewModel{{RunID: \"live-1\", Agent: \"builder\"}},\n\t\tRecentRuns: []RunViewModel{{RunID: \"recent-1\", ExitLabel: \"exit 0\", Status: \"passed\"}},\n\t}\n\tvar instanceHTML bytes.Buffer\n\tif err := templates.ExecuteTemplate(&instanceHTML, \"instance.html\", selected); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"render instance template: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\tfor _, want := range []string{`data-log-open`, `hx-target=\"#log-drawer-body\"`} {\n\t\tif !strings.Contains(instanceHTML.String(), want) {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"instance template missing %q\", want)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\texit := 0\n\tvar log bytes.Buffer\n\tif err := templates.ExecuteTemplate(&log, \"log.html\", LogView{RunID: \"run-1\", State: \"retained\", Text: \"line one\", Exit: &exit}); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"render log template: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\tfor _, want := range []string{`data-log-copy`, \"run-1\", \"line one\"} {\n\t\tif !strings.Contains(log.String(), want) {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"log template missing %q\", want)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788550200676},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now let me look at the full `instance.html` and `page.html` templates to understand the DOM structure and where I can add tests.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Now let me look at the full `instance.html` and `page.html` templates to understand the DOM structure and where I can add tests.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_8c00dce2d2b54ae2883700c2","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/phaedrus/development/misty-step/canopy/.forest/worktrees/1788550107325557414-fixer/templates/instance.html"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_533b181684114e53997cff03","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/phaedrus/development/misty-step/canopy/.forest/worktrees/1788550107325557414-fixer/templates/page.html"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731","usage":{"input":1959,"output":167,"cacheRead":33792,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":32,"totalTokens":35918,"cost":{"input":0.00009795000000000001,"output":0.000016700000000000003,"cacheRead":0.00033792,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00045257}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788550200678,"responseId":"gen-1788550200-0uYuGUDPp4Dir7zTpTeR","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_8c00dce2d2b54ae2883700c2","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<section class=\"instance-panel\" id=\"instance-panel\"\n  {{if .ID}}hx-get=\"/fragments/instance?instance={{.ID}}\" hx-trigger=\"every 2s [document.hidden === false]\" hx-target=\"#instance-panel\" hx-swap=\"outerHTML\"{{end}}>\n  {{if .ID}}\n    <header class=\"identity\">\n      <div>\n        <p class=\"eyebrow\">Selected instance</p>\n        <h2>{{if .Label}}{{.Label}}{{else}}{{.ID}}{{end}}</h2>\n        <p class=\"identity-meta\"><span class=\"mono\">{{.ID}}</span>{{if .Host}} · {{.Host}}{{end}}{{if .Repo}} · {{.Repo}}{{end}}</p>\n      </div>\n      <span class=\"status-badge {{.Freshness}}\">{{.Freshness}}</span>\n      {{if .LastObserved}}<span class=\"identity-meta\">last observed {{.LastObserved}}</span>{{end}}\n    </header>\n\n    {{if .Errors}}\n      <section class=\"card card-wide\" aria-labelledby=\"instance-errors\">\n        <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"instance-errors\">Collection warnings</h2><span class=\"signal error\">attention</span></div>\n        <ul class=\"error-list\">\n          {{range .Errors}}<li>{{.}}</li>{{end}}\n        </ul>\n      </section>\n    {{end}}\n\n    {{if eq .Freshness \"unknown\"}}\n      <section class=\"card card-wide\" aria-labelledby=\"waiting-title\">\n        <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"waiting-title\">Waiting for first observation</h2><span class=\"signal unknown\">unknown</span></div>\n        <p class=\"muted\">No successful snapshot is available yet. Canopy will keep trying without treating this instance as healthy.</p>\n      </section>\n    {{else}}\n      <div class=\"card-grid\">\n        <section class=\"card card-third\" aria-labelledby=\"overview-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"overview-title\">Overview</h2><span class=\"signal {{.Freshness}}\">{{.Freshness}}</span></div>\n          <dl class=\"metric-row\">\n            <div class=\"metric\"><dt>Kernel</dt><dd>{{if .KernelKnown}}{{if .KernelRunning}}running{{else}}stopped{{end}}{{else}}unknown{{end}}</dd></div>\n            <div class=\"metric\"><dt>Live runs</dt><dd>{{.ActiveRuns}}</dd></div>\n            <div class=\"metric\"><dt>Build</dt><dd class=\"mono\">{{.Version}}</dd></div>\n          </dl>\n        </section>\n        <section class=\"card card-third\" aria-labelledby=\"audit-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"audit-title\">Audit trust</h2><span class=\"signal {{.Audit.TrustClass}}\">{{.Audit.Trust}}</span></div>\n          <dl class=\"metric-row\">\n            <div class=\"metric\"><dt>Result</dt><dd>{{if .Audit.LastResult}}{{.Audit.LastResult}}{{else}}unknown{{end}}</dd></div>\n            <div class=\"metric\"><dt>Audited</dt><dd class=\"mono\">{{if .Audit.AuditedMaster}}{{.Audit.AuditedMaster}}{{else}}unknown{{end}}</dd></div>\n          </dl>\n          {{if .Audit.Violations}}<ul class=\"error-list\">{{range .Audit.Violations}}<li>{{.}}</li>{{end}}</ul>{{end}}\n        </section>\n        <section class=\"card card-third\" aria-labelledby=\"config-summary-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"config-summary-title\">Configuration</h2><span class=\"muted mono\">{{.Config.Primary}}</span></div>\n          <p class=\"muted\">{{if .Config.PrimarySource}}primary from {{.Config.PrimarySource}}{{else}}primary declaration{{end}}</p>\n          {{if .Config.Scope.Label}}<p><strong>Scope:</strong> {{.Config.Scope.Label}}</p>{{end}}\n          {{if .Config.Scope.BranchPrefix}}<p><strong>Branch:</strong> <span class=\"mono\">{{.Config.Scope.BranchPrefix}}</span></p>{{end}}\n          {{if .Config.Scope.Subjects}}<p><strong>Subjects:</strong> {{len .Config.Scope.Subjects}}</p>{{end}}\n        </section>\n\n        <section class=\"card card-wide\" aria-labelledby=\"live-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"live-title\">Live work</h2><span class=\"muted\">{{.ActiveRuns}} active</span></div>\n          {{if .LiveRuns}}\n            <div class=\"data-table-wrap\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Run</th><th>Agent</th><th>Started</th><th>Elapsed</th><th>Logs</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n              {{range .LiveRuns}}\n                <tr><td class=\"mono\">{{.RunID}}</td><td>{{if .Agent}}{{.Agent}}{{else}}legacy / unnamed{{end}}</td><td class=\"mono\">{{.StartedAt}}</td><td>{{.Elapsed}}</td><td><a class=\"log-link\" data-log-open href=\"/logs?instance={{$.ID}}&amp;run={{.RunID}}\" hx-get=\"/logs?instance={{$.ID}}&amp;run={{.RunID}}\" hx-target=\"#log-drawer-body\" hx-swap=\"innerHTML\">open log</a></td></tr>\n              {{end}}\n            </tbody></table></div>\n          {{else}}<p class=\"empty\">No live runs reported.</p>{{end}}\n        </section>\n\n        <section class=\"card card-half\" aria-labelledby=\"trigger-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"trigger-title\">Trigger matrix</h2><span class=\"muted\">poll vs run</span></div>\n          {{if .Triggers}}\n            <div class=\"data-table-wrap\">\n            <table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th>Poll</th><th>Run</th><th>Errors</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n              {{range .Triggers}}\n                <tr><td>{{if .Name}}{{.Name}}{{else}}legacy / unnamed{{end}}</td><td><span class=\"signal {{.PollStatus}}\">{{.PollStatus}}</span>{{if .PollError}}<br><span class=\"muted\">{{.PollError}}</span>{{end}}</td><td><span class=\"signal {{.RunStatus}}\">{{.RunStatus}}</span>{{if .RunError}}<br><span class=\"muted\">{{.RunError}}</span>{{end}}</td><td>{{.ConsecutiveErrors}}</td></tr>\n              {{end}}\n            </tbody></table>\n            </div>\n          {{else}}<p class=\"empty\">No trigger state reported.</p>{{end}}\n        </section>\n\n        <section class=\"card card-half\" aria-labelledby=\"recent-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"recent-title\">Recent runs</h2><span class=\"muted\">newest first</span></div>\n          {{if .RecentRuns}}\n            <div class=\"data-table-wrap\">\n            <table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Run</th><th>Agent</th><th>Outcome</th><th>Duration</th><th>Logs</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n              {{range .RecentRuns}}\n                <tr><td class=\"mono\">{{.RunID}}</td><td>{{if .Agent}}{{.Agent}}{{else}}legacy / unnamed{{end}}</td><td><span class=\"signal {{if eq .Status \"passed\"}}ok{{else}}error{{end}}\">{{.ExitLabel}}</span>{{if .Error}}<br><span class=\"muted\">{{.Error}}</span>{{end}}</td><td>{{.Duration}}</td><td><a class=\"log-link\" data-log-open href=\"/logs?instance={{$.ID}}&amp;run={{.RunID}}\" hx-get=\"/logs?instance={{$.ID}}&amp;run={{.RunID}}\" hx-target=\"#log-drawer-body\" hx-swap=\"innerHTML\">open</a></td></tr>\n              {{end}}\n            </tbody></table>\n            </div>\n          {{else}}<p class=\"empty\">No recent runs reported.</p>{{end}}\n        </section>\n\n        <section class=\"card card-wide\" aria-labelledby=\"ledger-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\">\n            <h2 id=\"ledger-title\">Ledger agents</h2>\n            {{if .HasLedger}}\n              <span class=\"muted\">{{.LedgerRuns}} runs · {{.LedgerPassRate}} pass rate</span>\n            {{else}}\n              <span class=\"signal unknown\">unsupported</span>\n            {{end}}\n          </div>\n          {{if .HasLedger}}\n            {{if .Agents}}\n              <div class=\"data-table-wrap\">\n              <table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th class=\"number\">Runs</th><th class=\"number\">Pass rate</th><th class=\"number\">P50</th><th class=\"number\">P95</th><th class=\"number\">Tokens in</th><th class=\"number\">Tokens out</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n                {{range .Agents}}\n                  <tr><td>{{if .Agent}}{{.Agent}}{{else}}legacy / unnamed{{end}}</td><td class=\"number\">{{.Runs}}</td><td class=\"number\">{{.PassRateLabel}}</td><td class=\"number\">{{.DurationP50Label}}</td><td class=\"number\">{{.DurationP95Label}}</td><td class=\"number\">{{.TokensIn}}</td><td class=\"number\">{{.TokensOut}}</td></tr>\n                {{end}}\n              </tbody></table>\n              </div>\n            {{else}}<p class=\"empty\">No Ledger agents recorded.</p>{{end}}\n          {{else}}\n            <p class=\"empty\">This Iron Forest build does not export status.ledger aggregates.</p>\n          {{end}}\n        </section>\n        <section class=\"card card-wide\" aria-labelledby=\"declarations-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"declarations-title\">Declarations</h2><span class=\"muted\">configuration snapshot</span></div>\n          {{if .Config.Declarations}}\n            <div class=\"data-table-wrap\">\n            <table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Model</th><th>Model source</th><th>Tools</th><th>Watchdog</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n              {{range .Config.Declarations}}\n                <tr><td>{{if .Name}}{{.Name}}{{else}}legacy / unnamed{{end}}</td><td class=\"mono\">{{.Model}}</td><td>{{.ModelSource}}</td><td>{{range $index, $tool := .Tools}}{{if $index}}, {{end}}{{$tool}}{{end}}</td><td>{{if .MaxDuration}}{{.MaxDuration}}s{{else}}unbounded{{end}}</td></tr>\n              {{end}}\n            </tbody></table>\n            </div>\n          {{else}}<p class=\"empty\">No declarations reported.</p>{{end}}\n        </section>\n      </div>\n    {{end}}\n  {{else}}\n    <section class=\"card card-wide\" aria-labelledby=\"empty-title\"><div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"empty-title\">No instance selected</h2><span class=\"signal unknown\">unknown</span></div><p class=\"empty\">Add an instance to the inventory to begin observing it.</p></section>\n  {{end}}\n</section>\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788550201929},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_533b181684114e53997cff03","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n  <title>Canopy · Iron Forest visibility</title>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"/static/canopy.css\">\n</head>\n<body>\n  <div class=\"app-shell\">\n    {{template \"fleet.html\" .}}\n    <main class=\"page-content\" id=\"main-content\">\n      <header class=\"page-header\">\n        <div>\n          <p class=\"eyebrow\">Read-only operations surface</p>\n          <h1>Canopy</h1>\n          <p class=\"lede\">A quiet, external view of the work your Iron Forest instances are doing now and what they last proved.</p>\n        </div>\n        <p class=\"muted mono\">forest.cli.v2</p>\n      </header>\n      {{template \"instance.html\" .Selected}}\n    </main>\n  </div>\n  <div id=\"log-drawer\" class=\"log-drawer\" role=\"dialog\" aria-modal=\"true\" aria-labelledby=\"log-drawer-title\" hidden>\n    <div class=\"log-drawer-backdrop\" data-log-close aria-hidden=\"true\"></div>\n    <section class=\"log-drawer-panel\" aria-label=\"Run log viewer\">\n      <header class=\"log-drawer-header\">\n        <h2 id=\"log-drawer-title\">Run log</h2>\n        <button type=\"button\" class=\"log-drawer-close\" data-log-close aria-label=\"Close log viewer\">Close</button>\n      </header>\n      <div id=\"log-drawer-body\" class=\"log-drawer-body\"></div>\n    </section>\n  </div>\n  <script src=\"/static/htmx.min.js\" defer></script>\n  <script src=\"/static/canopy.js\" defer></script>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788550201929},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a complete picture. Let me plan the fix.\n\n**The defect**: All log links (`[data-log-open]` in LiveRuns and RecentRuns tables) issue `hx-get` requests targeting the shared `#log-drawer-body` with `hx-swap=\"innerHTML\"`. There is no request serialization/abort and no stale-response guard. `handleLogs` can block up to `refreshTimeout` (30s). So clicking run A then run B before A's response returns can let A's slower response arrive last and overwrite B's log in the drawer — showing the wrong run's log.\n\n**Fix plan** (JS in `static/canopy.js`):\n\n1. Track the element that owns the in-flight log request (`logRequestElt`) and the currently-open trigger (`lastTrigger` — already exists).\n2. When opening the drawer for a new run: abort any in-flight log request from a previous run (request serialization via abort).\n3. Before any swap into the drawer: discard (preventDefault) the swap if the responding request does not belong to the currently-open run (stale-response guard).\n\nImplementation details in the vendored htmx:\n- `htmx.trigger(elt, \"htmx:abort\")` — the body-level delegated handler aborts `getInternalData(elt).xhr`. Without hx-sync, the xhr is stored on the requesting element itself. If the request already completed, xhr is null → no-op. Safe.\n\nWait, I need to double-check: `htmx:abort` handler: `e.detail.elt || e.target`. When we call `htmx.trigger(elt, \"htmx:abort\")`, htmx's trigger creates an event with `detail.elt` set to elt (in htmx, `triggerEvent` sets `detail.elt = elt` I believe) and dispatches on elt; it bubbles to body where the handler reads `e.detail.elt`, gets our elt, and aborts its xhr. Good.\n\n- `htmx:beforeSwap` — does preventDefault cancel the swap in this vendored version? Let me check the vendored code for beforeSwap handling.\n\nLet me verify: search for `beforeSwap` in the minified source with context.\n\nAlso I should think about whether the abort should happen in the click handler or in `htmx:beforeRequest`. The click handler (canopy.js's openDrawer path) runs at document level, AFTER htmx has already issued the new request (htmx listens on body). Hmm wait — actually order matters: if htmx issues B's request BEFORE our document-level click handler runs `openDrawer(B)` → in openDrawer we'd abort the previous request A. That works: abort A after B's request issues. Both A aborted and B proceeds. \n\nBut there's a subtle failure: `htmx:beforeRequest` for B runs during B's issue (in the body-level click handler). At that point, `logRequestElt` = A (set when A's request was issued via beforeRequest listener). Then our document click handler runs openDrawer(B) → aborts A and sets `logRequestElt = B`?? Wait no — openDrawer doesn't set logRequestElt; the beforeRequest listener sets it. Order at click time: htmx issues B (beforeRequest fires → set logRequestElt = B), then openDrawer runs → abort A. So by the time openDrawer runs, logRequestElt is already B. Then it aborts \"previous in-flight log request\" — we need to abort A, not B. So the abort must capture A before it's overwritten. Using `lastTrigger` (set on previous open) as the element to abort is more reliable: in openDrawer, before setting lastTrigger = new link, abort the in-flight request owned by the previous lastTrigger.\n\nSo:\n\n```js\nfunction openDrawer(trigger) {\n  var panel = drawer();\n  if (!panel) return;\n  var content = body();\n  if (content) {\n    content.innerHTML = '<p class=\"muted\">Loading log\\u2026</p>';\n  }\n  if (lastTrigger && lastTrigger !== trigger) {\n    abortLogRequest(lastTrigger);\n  }\n  lastTrigger = trigger || null;\n  panel.hidden = false;\n  ...\n}\n\nfunction abortLogRequest(elt) {\n  if (!elt || typeof htmx === \"undefined\") return;\n  htmx.trigger(elt, \"htmx:abort\");\n}\n```\n\nHmm — aborting a request whose xhr already completed is a no-op (xhr null). So aborting `lastTrigger` (the previously opened run's link) is safe even if its response already arrived.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety: `lastTrigger` may have been replaced in the DOM by an htmx swap. E.g., the instance panel refreshes every 2s (`hx-trigger=\"every 2s [document.hidden === false]\"` on `#instance-panel` with outerHTML swap). When the panel refreshes, the old link element is removed from the DOM; `lastTrigger` references a detached element. `htmx.trigger(detachedElt, \"htmx:abort\")` still works (events on detached elements still dispatch handlers attached via delegation? No — delegation via body listener means events dispatched on a detached element won't bubble to body since it's not in the DOM). Hmm.\n\nActually — an important consequence of the panel refresh: if run A's log request is in flight and the instance panel swaps with new rows (new link elements), the old A elt is detached — `getInternalData(A).xhr` still exists on the detached element, but aborting via trigger won't reach the body listener. BUT the stale-swap guard handles that case: at swap time for A, `lastTrigger` — hmm, if the panel refreshed, lastTrigger (old detached A elt) !== the current swap elt... wait the swap guard compares identity of the requesting elt (A) with lastTrigger — they're the same element object (the response swap uses the same request's elt reference). So the guard would pass even though the drawer content... Hmm, does the swap guard need to be \"the request was initiated from the currently open trigger\"? If the user opened A, panel refreshed (A's row re-rendered with a NEW element), and A's pending request (old elt) completes → swap guard sees elt === lastTrigger (both old A elt) → allows the swap. Content shows A's log — which IS what the user asked for. Fine, correct behavior.\n\nIf the user opened A, panel refreshed, then clicked B (new elt): openDrawer(B) → lastTrigger = B. A's stale response arrives → guard: elt (old A) !== lastTrigger (B) → discard. Correct!\n\nSo the swap guard is the critical correctness piece; the abort is an optimization to cancel the in-flight request promptly (also preferred so the server connection is dropped — actually aborting XHR drops the client side; the server may still process, but the response is discarded).\n\nNow, `htmx:beforeSwap` + `preventDefault`: need to verify it cancels the swap in this htmx version. Let me check the vendored code.\n\nAlso consider `htmx:beforeRequest` for setting a `logRequestElt`— not needed if we rely on lastTrigger.\n\nEdge case — closeDrawer: sets lastTrigger = null. If a response arrives after close, guard discards it (elt !== null). Also drawer hidden; content cleared. Good — improves behavior (no stale log loaded into hidden drawer). But wait — could this break the normal flow? Normal flow: open A → response swaps (guard: elt === lastTrigger(A) → allow). Then user closes → lastTrigger null. No more in-flight requests. OK.\n\nWhat about clicking the SAME link twice (A, then A again)? openDrawer(A): lastTrigger === A === trigger → no abort needed. fine.\n\nWhat about opening with modifier keys? Current handler: `if (event.button === 0 && !meta && !ctrl && !shift && !alt) { openDrawer(openLink); }` — otherwise it returns from the click handler. With a modifier click, htmx might still issue the request (htmx 2.x: for non-boosted links, it does handle modified clicks? Actually htmx respects `event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey` — let me check: in htmx 2.x, `shouldSkip`... hmm. Not important for the fix).\n\nNow let me also think about `hx-swap=\"innerHTML\"` with the guard. The guard attaches to `htmx:beforeSwap` events globally; filter on requesting elements that are log links: `event.detail.elt.closest(\"[data-log-open]\")` — but after the panel refresh, the elt is detached; `closest` still works on detached elements (walks ancestors within the detached subtree). The link always has `data-log-open` itself. OK.\n\nOne more: the `htmx:beforeRequest` event — is it fired for the log requests? Yes, before each request. But we don't need it. Actually — wait, do we want to abort the PREVIOUS request when a NEW request is issued directly (not via our click handler)? All log requests originate from clicks on `[data-log-open]` links (htmx click-triggered). Our click handler runs on every such click. So aborting in openDrawer covers all new-request paths. \n\nHmm, but hold on — one more subtle ordering issue I should double check: does our document-level click listener run before or after htmx's handler? htmx attaches its click handler to... let me check the vendored source: `e.addEventListener(\"click\", ...)` on what — `te().body` probably, or document. If htmx's listener is on body and ours on document, then during a click event: bubble phase: target → ... → body (htmx handles) → html → document (ours). So htmx issues B's request first, then openDrawer(B) aborts A. That's the order I assumed. But wait: what if htmx attaches to document too (with capture or on document in bubble phase, registered earlier)? Then ours (registered later on document) runs after htmx's document listener in bubble phase. Either way, htmx's issue happens before our openDrawer in the same event dispatch. Fine.\n\nNow — verify `htmx:beforeSwap` preventDefault semantics in the vendored htmx. Let me check.\n\nAdditionally — should I also add an `hx-indicator` or 'Loading' handling? The drawer already shows \"Loading log…\" when opened via openDrawer. If the request is aborted (replaced by a newer one), the newer openDrawer already set the loading placeholder. Good.\n\nAlso potential issue: the abort triggers XHR abort → htmx fires `htmx:responseError`? Not relevant.\n\nLet me check the `beforeSwap` implementation in vendored htmx.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Now I have a complete picture. Let me plan the fix.\\n\\n**The defect**: All log links (`[data-log-open]` in LiveRuns and RecentRuns tables) issue `hx-get` requests targeting the shared `#log-drawer-body` with `hx-swap=\\\"innerHTML\\\"`. There is no request serialization/abort and no stale-response guard. `handleLogs` can block up to `refreshTimeout` (30s). So clicking run A then run B before A's response returns can let A's slower response arrive last and overwrite B's log in the drawer — showing the wrong run's log.\\n\\n**Fix plan** (JS in `static/canopy.js`):\\n\\n1. Track the element that owns the in-flight log request (`logRequestElt`) and the currently-open trigger (`lastTrigger` — already exists).\\n2. When opening the drawer for a new run: abort any in-flight log request from a previous run (request serialization via abort).\\n3. Before any swap into the drawer: discard (preventDefault) the swap if the responding request does not belong to the currently-open run (stale-response guard).\\n\\nImplementation details in the vendored htmx:\\n- `htmx.trigger(elt, \\\"htmx:abort\\\")` — the body-level delegated handler aborts `getInternalData(elt).xhr`. Without hx-sync, the xhr is stored on the requesting element itself. If the request already completed, xhr is null → no-op. Safe.\\n\\nWait, I need to double-check: `htmx:abort` handler: `e.detail.elt || e.target`. When we call `htmx.trigger(elt, \\\"htmx:abort\\\")`, htmx's trigger creates an event with `detail.elt` set to elt (in htmx, `triggerEvent` sets `detail.elt = elt` I believe) and dispatches on elt; it bubbles to body where the handler reads `e.detail.elt`, gets our elt, and aborts its xhr. Good.\\n\\n- `htmx:beforeSwap` — does preventDefault cancel the swap in this vendored version? Let me check the vendored code for beforeSwap handling.\\n\\nLet me verify: search for `beforeSwap` in the minified source with context.\\n\\nAlso I should think about whether the abort should happen in the click handler or in `htmx:beforeRequest`. The click handler (canopy.js's openDrawer path) runs at document level, AFTER htmx has already issued the new request (htmx listens on body). Hmm wait — actually order matters: if htmx issues B's request BEFORE our document-level click handler runs `openDrawer(B)` → in openDrawer we'd abort the previous request A. That works: abort A after B's request issues. Both A aborted and B proceeds. \\n\\nBut there's a subtle failure: `htmx:beforeRequest` for B runs during B's issue (in the body-level click handler). At that point, `logRequestElt` = A (set when A's request was issued via beforeRequest listener). Then our document click handler runs openDrawer(B) → aborts A and sets `logRequestElt = B`?? Wait no — openDrawer doesn't set logRequestElt; the beforeRequest listener sets it. Order at click time: htmx issues B (beforeRequest fires → set logRequestElt = B), then openDrawer runs → abort A. So by the time openDrawer runs, logRequestElt is already B. Then it aborts \\\"previous in-flight log request\\\" — we need to abort A, not B. So the abort must capture A before it's overwritten. Using `lastTrigger` (set on previous open) as the element to abort is more reliable: in openDrawer, before setting lastTrigger = new link, abort the in-flight request owned by the previous lastTrigger.\\n\\nSo:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction openDrawer(trigger) {\\n  var panel = drawer();\\n  if (!panel) return;\\n  var content = body();\\n  if (content) {\\n    content.innerHTML = '<p class=\\\"muted\\\">Loading log\\\\u2026</p>';\\n  }\\n  if (lastTrigger && lastTrigger !== trigger) {\\n    abortLogRequest(lastTrigger);\\n  }\\n  lastTrigger = trigger || null;\\n  panel.hidden = false;\\n  ...\\n}\\n\\nfunction abortLogRequest(elt) {\\n  if (!elt || typeof htmx === \\\"undefined\\\") return;\\n  htmx.trigger(elt, \\\"htmx:abort\\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\nHmm — aborting a request whose xhr already completed is a no-op (xhr null). So aborting `lastTrigger` (the previously opened run's link) is safe even if its response already arrived.\\n\\nBut wait — there's a subtlety: `lastTrigger` may have been replaced in the DOM by an htmx swap. E.g., the instance panel refreshes every 2s (`hx-trigger=\\\"every 2s [document.hidden === false]\\\"` on `#instance-panel` with outerHTML swap). When the panel refreshes, the old link element is removed from the DOM; `lastTrigger` references a detached element. `htmx.trigger(detachedElt, \\\"htmx:abort\\\")` still works (events on detached elements still dispatch handlers attached via delegation? No — delegation via body listener means events dispatched on a detached element won't bubble to body since it's not in the DOM). Hmm.\\n\\nActually — an important consequence of the panel refresh: if run A's log request is in flight and the instance panel swaps with new rows (new link elements), the old A elt is detached — `getInternalData(A).xhr` still exists on the detached element, but aborting via trigger won't reach the body listener. BUT the stale-swap guard handles that case: at swap time for A, `lastTrigger` — hmm, if the panel refreshed, lastTrigger (old detached A elt) !== the current swap elt... wait the swap guard compares identity of the requesting elt (A) with lastTrigger — they're the same element object (the response swap uses the same request's elt reference). So the guard would pass even though the drawer content... Hmm, does the swap guard need to be \\\"the request was initiated from the currently open trigger\\\"? If the user opened A, panel refreshed (A's row re-rendered with a NEW element), and A's pending request (old elt) completes → swap guard sees elt === lastTrigger (both old A elt) → allows the swap. Content shows A's log — which IS what the user asked for. Fine, correct behavior.\\n\\nIf the user opened A, panel refreshed, then clicked B (new elt): openDrawer(B) → lastTrigger = B. A's stale response arrives → guard: elt (old A) !== lastTrigger (B) → discard. Correct!\\n\\nSo the swap guard is the critical correctness piece; the abort is an optimization to cancel the in-flight request promptly (also preferred so the server connection is dropped — actually aborting XHR drops the client side; the server may still process, but the response is discarded).\\n\\nNow, `htmx:beforeSwap` + `preventDefault`: need to verify it cancels the swap in this htmx version. Let me check the vendored code.\\n\\nAlso consider `htmx:beforeRequest` for setting a `logRequestElt`— not needed if we rely on lastTrigger.\\n\\nEdge case — closeDrawer: sets lastTrigger = null. If a response arrives after close, guard discards it (elt !== null). Also drawer hidden; content cleared. Good — improves behavior (no stale log loaded into hidden drawer). But wait — could this break the normal flow? Normal flow: open A → response swaps (guard: elt === lastTrigger(A) → allow). Then user closes → lastTrigger null. No more in-flight requests. OK.\\n\\nWhat about clicking the SAME link twice (A, then A again)? openDrawer(A): lastTrigger === A === trigger → no abort needed. fine.\\n\\nWhat about opening with modifier keys? Current handler: `if (event.button === 0 && !meta && !ctrl && !shift && !alt) { openDrawer(openLink); }` — otherwise it returns from the click handler. With a modifier click, htmx might still issue the request (htmx 2.x: for non-boosted links, it does handle modified clicks? Actually htmx respects `event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey` — let me check: in htmx 2.x, `shouldSkip`... hmm. Not important for the fix).\\n\\nNow let me also think about `hx-swap=\\\"innerHTML\\\"` with the guard. The guard attaches to `htmx:beforeSwap` events globally; filter on requesting elements that are log links: `event.detail.elt.closest(\\\"[data-log-open]\\\")` — but after the panel refresh, the elt is detached; `closest` still works on detached elements (walks ancestors within the detached subtree). The link always has `data-log-open` itself. OK.\\n\\nOne more: the `htmx:beforeRequest` event — is it fired for the log requests? Yes, before each request. But we don't need it. Actually — wait, do we want to abort the PREVIOUS request when a NEW request is issued directly (not via our click handler)? All log requests originate from clicks on `[data-log-open]` links (htmx click-triggered). Our click handler runs on every such click. So aborting in openDrawer covers all new-request paths. \\n\\nHmm, but hold on — one more subtle ordering issue I should double check: does our document-level click listener run before or after htmx's handler? htmx attaches its click handler to... let me check the vendored source: `e.addEventListener(\\\"click\\\", ...)` on what — `te().body` probably, or document. If htmx's listener is on body and ours on document, then during a click event: bubble phase: target → ... → body (htmx handles) → html → document (ours). So htmx issues B's request first, then openDrawer(B) aborts A. That's the order I assumed. But wait: what if htmx attaches to document too (with capture or on document in bubble phase, registered earlier)? Then ours (registered later on document) runs after htmx's document listener in bubble phase. Either way, htmx's issue happens before our openDrawer in the same event dispatch. Fine.\\n\\nNow — verify `htmx:beforeSwap` preventDefault semantics in the vendored htmx. Let me check.\\n\\nAdditionally — should I also add an `hx-indicator` or 'Loading' handling? The drawer already shows \\\"Loading log…\\\" when opened via openDrawer. If the request is aborted (replaced by a newer one), the newer openDrawer already set the loading placeholder. Good.\\n\\nAlso potential issue: the abort triggers XHR abort → htmx fires `htmx:responseError`? Not relevant.\\n\\nLet me check the `beforeSwap` implementation in vendored htmx.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me verify how the vendored htmx handles `htmx:beforeSwap` and the abort event delivery."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_191249c59f364d6288ecd54c","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/phaedrus/development/misty-step/canopy/.forest/worktrees/1788550107325557414-fixer && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport re\nsrc = open('static/htmx.min.js').read()\nfor m in re.finditer(r'beforeSwap', src):\n    s = max(0, m.start()-500)\n    e = min(len(src), m.end()+800)\n    print('...', src[s:e], '...')\n    print('====')\nEOF"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731","usage":{"input":3165,"output":2586,"cacheRead":35840,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":2443,"totalTokens":41591,"cost":{"input":0.00015825000000000002,"output":0.0002586,"cacheRead":0.0003584,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00077525}},"stopReason":"to