tably, in htmx's triggerEvent: `detail.elt = elt` — hmm no, in real htmx 2.x source:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction triggerEvent(elt, eventName, detail) {\\n  ...\\n  detail = detail == null ? {} : detail;\\n  detail.elt = elt;\\n  ...\\n}\\n```\\n\\nSo `detail.elt` = the dispatch element = target for beforeSwap. Hmm, BUT htmx's beforeSwap event in 2.x has `detail.requestConfig.elt` as the request origin? Actually wait — in htmx 2.x, beforeRequest/beforeSwap event details include `elt` (the element that made the request), and `target`... let me actually check htmx 2.0.9's source properly. I recall from htmx docs (2.0):\\n\\n`htmx:beforeSwap` — \\\"This event is triggered before a swap is done... The default behavior of this event is to swap... If you want to override this... `event.detail.shouldSwap`\\\"... The detail: `{elt, target, serverResponse, isError, xhr, shouldSwap, ...}`. Docs for beforeSwap in htmx 2.x list: `elt` — the element that made the request? Hmm, htmx 1.x docs: beforeSwap detail has `xhr`, `target`, `shouldSwap`, `serverResponse`, `isError`. And `elt`? Let me check the actual dispatch in htmx 2.0.9's unminified source:\\n\\nFrom htmx GitHub (v2.0.9), `src/htmx.js`:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction handleAjaxResponse(elt, xhr, target, prompt, requestConfig) {\\n    ...\\n    const eventInfo = makeEvent({ ... }, elt)\\n    ...\\n    if (!triggerEvent(target, 'htmx:beforeSwap', eventInfo)) return\\n```\\n\\nAnd `makeEvent`:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction makeEvent(detail, target) {\\n    makeEvent = function(detail, target) {\\n        if (detail == null) {\\n            detail = {}\\n        }\\n        detail.elt = target\\n        return detail\\n    }\\n    return makeEvent(detail, target)\\n}\\n```\\n\\nSo `beforeSwap.detail.elt = target` (the swap target) in htmx 2.0.9 too! And the request origin is `detail.requestConfig.elt`? Hmm wait — the eventInfo built for the response:\\n\\n```js\\nlet eventInfo = makeEvent({ xhr: xhr, target: target, requestConfig: requestConfig, pathInfo: {...}, select: responseSelect, boosted: boosted, etc: ..., isError: error }, elt)\\n```\\n\\nHmm — that sets `eventInfo.elt = elt` (the request origin, since makeEvent's second arg is `elt`)... wait I wrote `makeEvent(detail, target)` with `detail.elt = target`. If called as `makeEvent({...}, elt)` then `detail.elt = elt` = the request origin!\\n\\nBut the vendored minified code: `Vn(t, e)` where `t` = request origin, `e` = eventInfo (T). And `var m = le({shouldSwap:..., serverResponse:...}, e)` — `le(e, t)` copies `t` into `e`... wait, `le(e,t){for(const n in t){e[n]=t[n]}}` — copies keys from t (second arg) into e (first arg). `le({...}, e)` copies e's keys (xhr, target, requestConfig, etc, boosted, select, pathInfo) into the detail object. So `m.elt` — NOT set by le (T has no elt key).\\n\\nThen `ae(r, \\\"htmx:beforeSwap\\\", m)` — `ae(e, t, n)`: `n.elt = e` — sets m.elt = r = the DISPATCH element. So `m.elt` = r = swap target (at the time of dispatch: `let r = e.target` — I need to double-check that r is the swap target at the ae call).\\n\\nFrom Vn:\\n```js\\nfunction Vn(t, e) {          // t = origin elt, e = T\\n  const n = e.xhr;\\n  let r = e.target;          // r = swap target\\n  const o = e.etc;\\n  const i = e.select;\\n  if (!ae(t, \\\"htmx:beforeOnLoad\\\", e)) return;\\n  if (HX-Trigger...) ...\\n  ...\\n  if (T(n, /HX-Retarget:/i)) { e.target = Un(t, ...) }\\n  if (T(n, /HX-Reswap:/i)) { p = ... }\\n  var g = n.response;\\n  var m = le({shouldSwap: f, serverResponse: g, isError: a, ignoreTitle: h, selectOverride: d, swapOverride: p}, e);\\n  if (u.event && !ae(r, u.event, m)) return;\\n  if (!ae(r, \\\"htmx:beforeSwap\\\", m)) return;\\n```\\n\\n`r` was captured at function start as `e.target` — the swap target. So `detail.elt` for beforeSwap = swap target = `#log-drawer-body`. NOT the request origin.\\n\\nHmm interesting — so in THIS vendored htmx version (2.0.9 presumably), `htmx:beforeSwap.detail.elt` = the swap target. The request origin is available as `detail.requestConfig.elt`? Let me confirm requestConfig makes it into `m`: `le(m, e)` — m gets T's keys including requestConfig (C). C.elt = the origin link. So `event.detail.requestConfig.elt` identifies the link.\\n\\nHmm, this is getting fragile. Let me take a step back and design the guard to not depend on which element is in `detail.elt`:\\n\\nAlternative: use the swap TARGET as the discriminator. All log requests target `#log-drawer-body` — that's the only thing swapped into the drawer. But we need to know WHICH run the response is for. Options:\\n\\n(a) Read the response: `event.detail.serverResponse` — the HTML fragment contains `<span class=\\\"log-run-id mono\\\">{{.RunID}}</span>`. Parse the run id from the response text: `data-log-run=\\\"...\\\"`? No — the response is log.html which contains `.log-run-id` span with the run id text. E.g. response: `<span class=\\\"log-run-id mono\\\">live-1</span>`. I could compare that against lastRun: extract via regex `/class=\\\"log-run-id mono\\\">([^<]+)</`. Hmm — brittle markup coupling, but it's OUR markup. Actually cleaner: add `data-log-run=\\\"...\\\"` to the log.html root (`<div class=\\\"log-pane\\\" data-log-run=\\\"{{.RunID}}\\\">`) and parse `data-log-run=\\\"([^\\\"]*)\\\"` from the response. Then at beforeSwap: if the response's data-log-run !== lastRun → discard. This uses only the response content + target identity — no dependence on detail.elt semantics.\\n\\n(b) Use `event.detail.requestConfig.elt` — depends on internal detail shape.\\n\\n(c) Use `event.target` / the dispatched element (`event.currentTarget` won't help since it's document). The event dispatches on the target (#log-drawer-body) and bubbles to document; `event.target` = #log-drawer-body. So: a swap targeting #log-drawer-body is a log swap. For identifying the run, parse the response.\\n\\nActually — hmm, let me reconsider (a) more: parsing the response HTML in JS. The response is: \\n\\n```html\\n<div class=\\\"log-pane\\\">\\n  <div class=\\\"log-pane-meta\\\">\\n    <span class=\\\"log-run-id mono\\\">live-1</span>\\n    ...\\n```\\n\\n`data-log-run` on the log-pane root would be the cleanest marker. But parsing via regex on serverResponse string is a bit clunky; alternatively, at beforeSwap time the response isn't in the DOM yet.\\n\\nHmm — actually WAIT. Let me reconsider the whole approach. There's a MUCH simpler, fully robust, and idiomatic htmx mechanism I overlooked: `hx-indicator`... no. What about the `htmx:beforeRequest` / `htmx:afterRequest` tracking:\\n\\nKeep a module-level `pendingRun` — set at `htmx:beforeRequest` when the request elt is a log link, cleared at `htmx:afterRequest` — then at `htmx:beforeSwap`... but the swap needs the RUN identity of the swapping response, which we know only via the request elt.\\n\\nThe cleanest identity carrier that survives everything: put `data-log-run` on the REQUEST element (done) and ALSO on the RESPONSE root (`log.html`), then in beforeSwap compare response marker to the drawer's expected run. The request-elt-based abort (openDrawer) handles prompt cancellation. The response-marker guard handles all late-arrival cases.\\n\\nActually hold on, hold on. Let me reconsider using `htmx:beforeSwap` at all. What about `htmx:beforeRequest`-based SERIALIZATION instead? In htmx 2.x, beforeRequest is dispatched on the REQUESTING element? Let me check: in the issueRequest code — `const G={target:c,elt:r,...}` for htmx:confirm — dispatched via `ae(r,\\\"htmx:confirm\\\",G)` on r (the elt). For beforeRequest: htmx docs say `htmx:beforeRequest` is dispatched on the element that made the request: `ae(r, \\\"htmx:beforeRequest\\\", C)` presumably (C includes elt: r). And `detail.elt = r` via ae's `n.elt = e`. So `htmx:beforeRequest.detail.elt` = the LINK. That matches the abort handler's expectation too.\\n\\nSo the abort-on-new-request approach: I could move the abort from openDrawer's click handler to `htmx:beforeRequest`, using `detail.elt` = the new link. Track `activeLogElt`:\\n\\n```js\\ndocument.addEventListener(\\\"htmx:beforeRequest\\\", function (event) {\\n  var elt = event.detail && event.detail.elt;\\n  if (!(elt && elt.closest && elt.closest(\\\"[data-log-open]\\\"))) return;\\n  if (activeLogElt && activeLogElt !== elt) cancelLogRequest(activeLogElt);\\n  activeLogElt = elt;\\n});\\n```\\n\\nHmm — wait, but I need to double check `beforeRequest.detail.elt` is the link in this vendored version. From the dump: `C = {boosted: $, ..., elt: r, target: c, verb: t, ...}` and then presumably `ae(r, \\\"htmx:beforeRequest\\\", C)`? Let me check what happens with C after its construction. Search for `htmx:beforeRequest`.\\n\\nAnd for the swap guard — combine: track `activeLogElt`/`lastRun`; at beforeSwap, the event dispatch element is the TARGET (#log-drawer-body). I can filter by `event.detail.requestConfig && event.detail.requestConfig.elt` having `[data-log-open]`, then compare that elt's run against lastRun.\\n\\nHmm, honestly — the SIMPLEST robust approach avoiding all detail-shape uncertainty: attach the BEFORE-SWAP GUARD to the htmx swap via `htmx:beforeSwap` but identify staleness EMPIRICALLY from the response + expected run:\\n\\n1. log.html response root gets `data-log-run=\\\"{{.RunID}}\\\"`.\\n2. `lastRun` tracks the opened run.\\n3. beforeSwap listener (fires for all swaps; dispatched on target):\\n   - If `event.target` (or `event.detail.target`) is `#log-drawer-body`... hmm wait — again the detail shape. But `event.target` from the bubbling event object IS reliable: the event was dispatched on r (the target); when it reaches document, event.target = r = the swap target. That's standard DOM. So: `if (event.target && event.target.id === \\\"log-drawer-body\\\")` identifies log swaps without relying on htmx internals.\\n   - Parse `data-log-run=\\\"([^\\\"]*)\\\"` from `event.detail.serverResponse` (serverResponse is a string — standard beforeSwap detail) — compare to lastRun; mismatch → preventDefault.\\n\\nHmm, wait — but what identifies OTHER swaps? `event.target.id === \\\"log-drawer-body\\\"` — only log requests swap there. \\n\\nNow — is `event.detail.serverResponse` reliably the response string in this version? From the dump: `m = le({shouldSwap: f, serverResponse: g, ...}, e)` → yes, serverResponse = n.response (the response text). And it's a raw string (non-transformed? `if(m.shouldSwap){... Vt(t, function(e){...transformResponse...})` — transformation happens AFTER beforeSwap). So at beforeSwap, serverResponse is the raw text. The log.html response contains `data-log-run=\\\"live-1\\\"`.\\n\\nThis design:\\n- Doesn't depend on `detail.elt` semantics (uses event.target + serverResponse, both standard/certain).\\n- Abort on open (prompt cancellation) — via the click handler as before.\\n- Stale-swap discard — via response marker vs lastRun.\\n\\nBut hmm — one more thought — using the explicit run marker on BOTH the request link AND the response root is more code but deterministic and testable: template test asserts `data-log-run` on log.html root too.\\n\\nAlternatively, KEEP the detail.elt approach but verify it against a REAL DOM test... no JS test infra. Given the minified code says beforeSwap dispatches on the target with detail.elt=target, my current `event.detail.elt` reads the TARGET, whose getAttribute(\\\"data-log-run\\\") is null ≠ lastRun → would DISCARD ALL log swaps! That would break the drawer entirely. Must fix.\\n\\nWait, actually, let me triple-check the dispatch element for beforeSwap — `ae(r, \\\"htmx:beforeSwap\\\", m)` where `r = e.target`. Is `e.target` the swap target or the ORIGIN? T was created as `{xhr: g, target: c, requestConfig: C, ...}` where `c` = `i.targetOverride || closest(elt, \\\"hx-target\\\")...` — from the issueRequest: `const c = i.targetOverride || ce(Ee(r))` — the resolved swap target. Then later in Vn: `if (u.target) { e.target = Un(t, u.target) }` (HX-Retarget) — still the target. So yes: r = swap target = #log-drawer-body for log requests. `detail.elt` = #log-drawer-body. Confirmed by code reading.\\n\\nBUT — hmm, wait. Let me reconsider: htmx 2.0.9's ACTUAL makeEvent... In real htmx 2.0.9 source (I should double check against known facts): htmx docs for `htmx:beforeSwap` say detail: `{elt:  the element that made the request, target: ..., shouldSwap, serverResponse, isError, xhr, ...}`? Let me actually check the htmx docs for 2.0: https://htmx.org/events/#htmx:beforeSwap — \\\"elt - the element that dispatched the request\\\"... Hmm, for beforeRequest: \\\"htmx:beforeRequest — ... detail: elt (the element that made the request)\\\". For beforeSwap, htmx docs: `elt` — hmm.\\n\\nGiven the ambiguity, the safe approach: don't read detail.elt in beforeSwap. Use event.target (bubbled dispatch origin — deterministic DOM semantics).\\n\\nHmm — actually wait, there's still ambiguity: is event.target at document = the dispatch element? The event is created with `new Event(name, {bubbles:true, cancelable:true})` and dispatched on r. Bubbling: r → ... → document. At document, event.target = r (dispatch origin). Yes, deterministic.\\n\\nBut hold on — what about htmx's internal re-dispatch with `Xt(i, r.detail)`? In ae: after dispatching on e, `if (o && i !== t) { const s = Xt(i, r.detail); o = o && e.dispatchEvent(s); }` — dispatches ANOTHER event `i` (eventName from Bt(t)) on e with same detail. Bt maps \\\"htmx:beforeSwap\\\" → ? If it re-dispatches as \\\"htmx:beforeSwap\\\" again on e... it's dispatched on the same element e, still bubbling to document with event.target = e. No issue.\\n\\nOK let me also double-check `document.addEventListener(\\\"htmx:beforeSwap\\\", ...)` ordering vs closeDrawer setting lastRun=null. Sequence for the LEGITIMATE case: open A → lastRun=\\\"A\\\" → response arrives → beforeSwap on #log-drawer-body → event.target.id === \\\"log-drawer-body\\\" → parse response marker \\\"A\\\" === lastRun \\\"A\\\" → allow. Correct.\\n\\nHmm, wait — actually, let me simplify even further. Since `event.detail.serverResponse` is the raw HTML text, and the response is OUR log.html — parse `data-log-run=\\\"...\\\"`:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction responseRunID(response) {\\n  var m = response && response.match(/data-log-run=\\\"([^\\\"]*)\\\"/);\\n  return m ? m[1] : null;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nCareful: `data-log-run=\\\"live-1\\\"` — the attribute appears in the response root div. Escaping: RunID goes through html/template escaping — quoted attribute values escape `\\\"` as `&#34;` and `&` as `&amp;`. In the RESPONSE, the rendered attribute is the escaped form; JS `.match(/data-log-run=\\\"([^\\\"]*)\\\"/)` reads the escaped text back — e.g. run id \\\"a&b\\\" renders as `data-log-run=\\\"a&amp;b\\\"` — after matching we get the ESCAPED string \\\"a&amp;b\\\" ≠ lastRun (\\\"a&b\\\" from the link's getAttribute — which returns the UNESCAPED value \\\"a&b\\\"). MISMATCH → legitimate response discarded!\\n\\nHmm. Wait — is that right? The link's `data-log-run=\\\"{{.RunID}}\\\"` renders as `data-log-run=\\\"a&amp;b\\\"` in HTML; `getAttribute(\\\"data-log-run\\\")` returns the decoded value `a&b`. The response's marker (from serverResponse text) is the raw HTML `a&amp;b`; regex extracts `a&amp;b`. So comparison fails for run ids containing `&` or `\\\"` or `<`.\\n\\nIs `data-log-run` in log.html necessary then? What run ids are valid? Let me check runParam validation in server.go — probably restricts run ids to safe chars (mono display). Let me check. If run ids are restricted to `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` etc., escaping is a non-issue. Let me check `runParam`.\\n\\nAlternatively — hmm, this is getting complicated. Let me step back and pick the approach with the least fragility:\\n\\n**Approach: track the request element at `htmx:beforeRequest`, guard at `htmx:beforeSwap` using `event.detail.requestConfig.elt`.**\\n\\nActually wait — even simpler: we don't need the response marker at all if we track the ACTIVE REQUEST's element and compare identity at swap time... but at beforeSwap we need the request elt for the RESPONSE. If I track `activeLogElt` (set in beforeRequest from detail.elt), then in beforeSwap I need the response's request elt: `event.detail.requestConfig.elt`. requestConfig is present in the merged detail (I saw `requestConfig` in T). Let me confirm `requestConfig.elt` is the link: C = requestConfig = `{... elt: r, target: c, ...}` — YES (from the earlier dump: `const C={boosted:$,useUrlParams:E,...,elt:r,target:c,...}`).\\n\\nHmm, but honestly, `requestConfig` feels like an internal detail that could change across htmx versions. It IS what this vendored version uses though, and the version is pinned (vendored htmx.min.js). The tests pin the wiring only loosely.\\n\\nUgh — OK let me think about which is LEAST fragile and most defensible:\\n\\nOption A (response marker): log.html root gets `data-log-run`; beforeSwap discards when response marker ≠ lastRun. Fragility: HTML-entity escaping (mitigate by restricting run id charset — check runParam), regex parsing of our own markup (deterministic since we write it).\\n\\nOption B (requestConfig.elt): identity/run comparison via detail.requestConfig.elt getAttribute(\\\"data-log-run\\\"). Fragility: relies on htmx internal detail key. No escaping issue (getAttribute decodes).\\n\\nOption C (element identity, no run values): at beforeRequest, set `activeLogElt = detail.elt`; at beforeSwap, read `event.detail.requestConfig.elt` — if it !== activeLogElt → discard. Requires detail.elts... Ehh.\\n\\nHmm, wait — actually, hold on. Let me reconsider. What does htmx 2.0.9's REAL (unminified) source do for beforeSwap? Let me fetch htmx 2.0.9 source from the vendored file... The vendored file IS 2.0.9 (per the earlier verdict referencing htmx 2.0.9; let me verify the version string). I can check real htmx 2.0.9 docs. From htmx 2.0 reference (https://htmx.org/events/):\\n\\n- `htmx:beforeRequest` — \\\"This event fires before an ajax request is made... detail: elt, target, path, verb, parameters, headers, ...\\\"\\n- `htmx:beforeSwap` — \\\"This event is triggered before a swap is done. Note that this event is triggered on the target of the swap (not the element making the request)...\\\" — I believe the docs mention it fires on the target. Detail includes `xhr, target, requestConfig, elt`? Hmm.\\n\\nActually, htmx 1.x beforeSwap detail: `{xhr, target, shouldSwap, serverResponse, isError}`. htmx 2.0 moved to `{xhr, target, requestConfig, ...}` plus others. The `elt` in the event detail for beforeSwap... In htmx 2.0's source:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction handleAjaxResponse(elt, xhr, target, prompt, requestConfig) {\\n    ...\\n    var eventInfo = {...};\\n    ...\\n    if (!triggerEvent(target, 'htmx:beforeSwap', eventInfo)) return;\\n```\\n\\nAnd triggerEvent sets `detail.elt = elt` — the dispatch target. Hmm wait, in htmx 2.0.9 source triggerEvent:\\n\\n```js\\nfunction triggerEvent(elt, eventName, detail) {\\n    elt = resolveTarget(elt) || document.body\\n    detail = detail == null ? {} : detail\\n    detail.elt = elt\\n    ...dispatches on elt...\\n}\\n```\\n\\nSo yes — in real htmx 2.0.9, beforeSwap's detail.elt = the target element (dispatch element), NOT the request origin! This matches my reading of the vendored minified code. (htmx docs for beforeSwap actually say: \\\"elt - the element that made the request\\\"? Hmm, but source says otherwise... The htmx docs for beforeSwap list `shouldSwap` primarily; official docs might not even list `elt`.)\\n\\nHmm OK. But WAIT — htmx's `resolveTarget`... and importantly, htmx 2.x ALSO exposes the request origin in the event detail via `requestConfig`? In real 2.0.9:\\n\\n```js\\nconst eventInfo = {\\n    xhr: xhr,\\n    target: target,\\n    requestConfig: requestConfig,\\n    pathInfo: pathInfo,\\n    etc: etc,\\n    boosted: boosted,\\n    select: responseSelect,\\n    isError: error,\\n}\\n```\\n\\nYes — `requestConfig` is in beforeSwap detail, and `requestConfig.elt` is the origin. Interesting — htmx 1.9.2 added `requestConfig` to events specifically so handlers could access the config of completed requests. So `event.detail.requestConfig.elt` is supported API surface (documented as part of the event detail in htmx 1.9.2+ / 2.0). \\n\\nHmm, wait, I realize I should just verify against the vendored file what I can and then write the guard to use ONLY well-established things:\\n\\n1. Abort: in openDrawer click handler — uses `lastTrigger` element + `htmx.trigger(elt, \\\"htmx:abort\\\")`. Solid (public API; abort handler verified).\\n2. Stale-swap guard: `htmx:beforeSwap` — filter log swaps by `event.target` — hmm, wait: `event.target` at document = dispatch element = the swap target (#log-drawer-body). So `event.target === body()` identifies log-drawer swaps. Then read run identity from `event.detail.requestConfig.elt` if present... \\n\\nActually you know what? Let me just verify the actual behavior with a REAL browser-like test using node + a minimal DOM shim? No jsdom available... Let me check if node exists and whether a simple Event/Element shim could exercise this. That's a lot of work.\\n\\nLet me choose the most defensible combination:\\n\\n**Final design**:\\n- `openDrawer` sets `lastRun = runID(trigger)` (from the link's data-log-run) and aborts the previous in-flight request via `htmx.trigger(lastTrigger, \\\"htmx:abort\\\")`.\\n- `htmx:beforeSwap` guard: \\n  ```js\\n  document.addEventListener(\\\"htmx:beforeSwap\\\", function (event) {\\n    var target = event.detail && event.detail.target;\\n    if (!target || target.id !== \\\"log-drawer-body\\\") return;   // only drawer swaps\\n    var requestElt = event.detail.requestConfig && event.detail.requestConfig.elt;\\n    if (!requestElt || runID(requestElt) !== lastRun) {\\n      event.preventDefault();\\n    }\\n  });\\n  ```\\n  - `event.detail.target` — the target element (in T: `target: c`; possibly updated by HX-Retarget). `target.id === \\\"log-drawer-body\\\"`